// Study Room content — generated from the HZT4U slide decks
window.LESSONS = [{"u": 1, "l": 1, "title": "What Is Philosophy?", "sub": "Our compass for the whole course — starting today.", "cards": [{"k": "notes", "h": "Defining Philosophy", "lbl": null, "b": ["Philosophy: the systematic study of fundamental questions about existence, knowledge, values, reason, mind, and language.", "It is not the same as “having an opinion.” Philosophy is a disciplined practice of questioning, arguing, and reasoning toward understanding.", "Philosophy often asks the questions other disciplines take for granted.", "e.g. Science assumes the world can be known — philosophy asks: how do we know that?"], "c": [{"t": "THINK ABOUT IT", "b": ["“Philosophy is questions that may never be answered, and Religion is answers that may never be questioned.” — anonymous"]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Philosophia: The Love of Wisdom", "lbl": null, "b": ["Greek roots:", "Philo (φίλος) = love / friendship", "Sophia (σοφία) = wisdom", "philosophia = “the love of wisdom”", "Associated with Pythagoras (6th century BCE), who reportedly refused the title “sophos” (a wise man) and called himself a “philosophos” instead — a lover of wisdom, since true wisdom belongs only to the gods.", "Key idea: philosophy is a pursuit, not a possession — the love OF wisdom, not the having of it."], "c": []}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Is Philosophy Something You Learn, or Something You Do?", "lbl": null, "b": ["If philosophy is a pursuit rather than a possession, can you ever “finish” it?", "Can someone be a philosopher without ever reading a philosophy book?", "Compare memorizing facts about wisdom vs. practicing the love of wisdom. Which one is philosophy?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Our Course Compass", "lbl": null, "b": ["“Be Curious First, Not Critical” — our guiding mantra for this entire course.", "Curiosity asks:", "“What's interesting or true about this idea? What is this person really getting at?”", "Criticism (too early) asks:", "“What's wrong with this? How do I win this argument?”", "This doesn't mean we never evaluate ideas — it means we understand first, and judge second.", "You will hear this mantra all year. When a discussion gets heated, come back to it."], "c": []}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Curiosity vs. Criticism", "lbl": null, "b": ["Recall a time someone criticized your idea before they understood it. How did that feel?", "Name one belief you hold that you've never really examined. Could you be curious about it today?", "Ground-rule check: what would “curious, not critical” actually look like in THIS classroom?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "The Big Questions of Philosophy", "lbl": null, "b": ["1. What is a Person?", "2. What is a Meaningful Life?", "3. What is Beauty?", "4. What are Good and Evil?", "5. What is a Just Society?", "6. What can be known?", "7. Can a machine think — and does it matter?"], "c": [{"t": "NOTE", "b": ["None of these questions have one correct answer. Different branches of philosophy — which we meet next lesson — give us different tools for approaching them."]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "What is a Person?", "lbl": "BIG QUESTION 1 OF 7", "b": ["Candidates for “what makes something a person”: consciousness, rationality, continuity of memory, a body, a soul, social relationships.", "Boethius (6th c. CE) defined a person as “an individual substance of a rational nature.”", "Modern puzzle: are “persons” the same thing as “human beings”? Could a non-human animal, or a future AI, be a person?"], "c": [{"t": "DISCUSS", "b": ["Is a newborn a person? A person in a coma? Why or why not?", "If your memories were erased tomorrow, would you still be “you”?"]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "What is a Meaningful Life?", "lbl": "BIG QUESTION 2 OF 7", "b": ["Hedonism: a meaningful life is a pleasurable / happy one.", "Eudaimonia (Aristotle): meaning = flourishing — living well and virtuously, not just feeling good.", "Existentialism: meaning is not given to us — it is created through our choices."], "c": [{"t": "DISCUSS", "b": ["Could a life be meaningful even if it wasn't happy?", "Do you create meaning, or discover it?"]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "What is beauty?", "lbl": "BIG QUESTION 3 OF 7", "b": ["Objectivism: beauty is a real property of things (Plato: beauty is a Form we recognize).", "Subjectivism: “beauty is in the eye of the beholder” — beauty is our reaction, not a property of the object.", "Kant's middle position: beauty involves a special pleasure we expect others to share, even though it can't be strictly proven."], "c": [{"t": "DISCUSS", "b": ["Can something be beautiful if literally no one ever sees it?", "Is there a difference between “I like this” and “this is beautiful”?"]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "What are Good and Evil?", "lbl": "BIG QUESTION 4 OF 7", "b": ["Moral Realism: good and evil are objective facts, true independent of opinion.", "Moral Relativism: they depend on culture, context, or the individual.", "Divine Command Theory: good = what a god commands.", "Consequentialism: good = what produces the best outcomes.", "Deontology: good = following the right duties, regardless of outcome."], "c": [{"t": "DISCUSS", "b": ["Is there any act that's wrong in every culture, at every time? What would that tell us?", "Can something be evil even if everyone involved believes it's good?"]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "What is a Just Society?", "lbl": "BIG QUESTION 5 OF 7", "b": ["Justice as fairness (Rawls): imagine designing society without knowing your place in it — the “veil of ignorance.”", "Justice as giving each person what they deserve (Aristotle) vs. what they need vs. what they've earned.", "Ongoing tension: individual liberty vs. collective equality."], "c": [{"t": "DISCUSS", "b": ["Behind Rawls' “veil of ignorance,” what rules would you build into society?", "Is a society just if it's efficient but unequal? Equal but poor?"]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "What can be known?", "lbl": "BIG QUESTION 6 OF 7", "b": ["Epistemology: the study of knowledge — what it is, and how (or whether) we can have it.", "Traditional definition: knowledge = “justified true belief.”", "Skepticism: radical doubt about whether we can know anything for certain (Descartes: “How do I know I'm not dreaming?”).", "Rationalism (reason) vs. Empiricism (experience) as sources of knowledge."], "c": [{"t": "DISCUSS", "b": ["Name one thing you are 100% certain of. Can you defend that certainty?", "If you can't be certain of something, should you stop believing it?"]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Can a machine think — and does it matter?", "lbl": "BIG QUESTION 7 OF 7", "b": ["A 21st-century Big Question — it forces us to revisit ALL the others: personhood, knowledge, meaning, ethics, justice.", "Alan Turing (1950) reframed the question: not “can machines think?” but “can machines do what we, as thinking beings, can do?” (the Turing Test).", "John Searle's “Chinese Room” objection: manipulating symbols correctly isn't the same as understanding them."], "c": [{"t": "DISCUSS", "b": ["What would actually convince you a machine was thinking, not just simulating thinking?", "If an AI could suffer, would that change how we're obligated to treat it — even if we can't be sure?"]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "One Set of Questions, Many Tools", "lbl": null, "b": ["Notice: none of today's questions got a single “correct” answer from the front of the room — that was intentional.", "Philosophy doesn't just supply answers. It supplies better questions and sharper tools for thinking.", "Next lesson: the Branches of Philosophy", "Metaphysics, Epistemology, and Ethics — the specific “toolkits” philosophers use to tackle questions exactly like today's.", "Remember the compass: Be Curious First, Not Critical."], "c": []}]}, {"u": 1, "l": 2, "title": "The Branches of Philosophy", "sub": "The toolkits philosophers use to tackle the Big Questions.", "cards": [{"k": "notes", "h": "The Branches of Philosophy", "lbl": null, "b": ["MetaphysicsWhat is real? (existence, mind, free will, time, God)", "EpistemologyWhat can be known, and how? (knowledge, belief, truth)", "VALUE THEORY", "EthicsWhat is good or right action? (morality, values, how to live)", "AestheticsWhat is beauty? (Is there an objective “Beautiful”, or is it always subjective?)", "Social & Political Philosophy — light touchWhat do we owe each other, as a society? (justice, rights, authority)", "Philosophy of Science — light touchWhat makes something count as “scientific” knowledge?"], "c": [{"t": "BONUS BRANCH", "b": ["Aesthetics asks “what is beauty/art?” — home of Lesson 1's Big Question 3. We won't dive deep into it this unit, but now you know its name."]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Metaphysics", "lbl": null, "b": ["From Greek meta (beyond) + physika (nature/physics) — literally “beyond the physical.”", "Central Question: what is real, and what is the fundamental nature of reality?", "Sample metaphysical questions: Do you have free will, or is everything determined? What makes you the same person you were 10 years ago? Is the mind the same thing as the brain? Does God exist?", "Metaphysics often asks about things science can't directly test or measure."], "c": [{"t": "KEY TERM", "b": ["Ontology — the sub-branch of metaphysics specifically about what exists, and the basic categories of being."]}]}, {"k": "watch", "h": "“Leonardo DiCaprio & The Nature of Reality”", "lbl": null, "b": ["Crash Course Philosophy #4 (Hank Green)   ·   ~10 min"], "c": [{"t": "WHILE YOU WATCH — JOT DOWN:", "b": ["Explain Plato's Allegory (Myth) of the Cave in your own words.", "What do the shadows on the wall represent?", "What does escaping the cave represent?"]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Plato's Cave, Today", "lbl": null, "b": ["Can you think of a modern “cave” — something that shows us shadows instead of reality? (social media? news? something else?)", "If someone escaped the cave and saw the truth, why might the people still inside resist believing them?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Epistemology", "lbl": null, "b": ["From Greek episteme (knowledge) + logos (study of) — “the study of knowledge.”", "Central question: what can we know, and how do we know it?", "Recall Lesson 1: knowledge = “justified true belief” (JTB); Descartes' skeptical doubt (“How do I know I'm not dreaming?”).", "Two historical camps on WHERE knowledge comes from:", "Rationalism — primarily through reason (e.g. Descartes)", "Empiricism — primarily through sense experience (e.g. Locke)"], "c": [{"t": "KEY TERM", "b": ["A priori: known independent of experience (e.g. math).", "A posteriori: known through experience (e.g. “it's raining”)."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Where Does Your Knowledge Come From?", "lbl": null, "b": ["Give one thing you know a priori and one thing you know a posteriori.", "Can reason alone tell us anything true about the world, without any experience at all?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Ethics", "lbl": null, "b": ["From Greek ethos (character/custom) — the study of right and wrong action, and how we should live.", "Three major frameworks for deciding what's right:", "Consequentialism (incl. Utilitarianism) — an act is right if it produces the best overall outcome", "Deontology (Kant) — an act is right if it follows the correct moral duty/rule, regardless of outcome", "Virtue Ethics (Aristotle) — an act is right if it's what a person of good character would do"], "c": [{"t": "KEY TERM — CALLBACK", "b": ["Remember eudaimonia from Lesson 1? It lives inside Virtue Ethics — flourishing through virtuous action, not just following rules or chasing outcomes."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "The Trolley Problem", "lbl": null, "b": ["A runaway trolley will kill 5 people unless you pull a lever, diverting it to a track where it will kill 1 person instead. What would a Consequentialist say to do? What would a Deontologist say?", "Does your own gut reaction match either framework, or neither?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Social & Political Philosophy", "lbl": null, "b": ["Central question: how should society be organized, and what do we owe each other?", "Key idea — Social Contract Theory: legitimate political authority comes from an (implicit) agreement among people to form a society and give up some freedoms in exchange for security and order (Hobbes, Locke, Rousseau).", "Recall Lesson 1, Big Question 5: Rawls' “veil of ignorance” is a modern social contract thought experiment."], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Philosophy of Science", "lbl": null, "b": ["Central Question: what makes something count as “scientific” knowledge?", "The Demarcation Problem: how do we tell science apart from non-science (or pseudoscience)?", "Karl Popper's Proposed Solution: a theory is scientific only if it's falsifiable — it must be possible, at least in principle, to prove it wrong."], "c": []}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Rules & Rigor", "lbl": null, "b": ["Would you agree to Rawls' rules for society from behind the veil of ignorance? Why or why not?", "Give an example of a claim that is NOT falsifiable. Why does that matter?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Mapping the Big Questions to the Branches", "lbl": null, "b": ["Q1 What is a Person?  →  Metaphysics (and a little Ethics)", "Q2 What is a Meaningful Life?  →  Ethics", "Q3 What is Beauty?  →  Aesthetics (bonus branch)", "Q4 What are Good and Evil?  →  Ethics", "Q5 What is a Just Society?  →  Social & Political Philosophy", "Q6 What can be known?  →  Epistemology", "Q7 Can a machine think?  →  Metaphysics + Epistemology (and Ethics, once we ask how to treat it)"], "c": [{"t": "NOTE", "b": ["Many Big Questions actually draw on more than one branch at once. Branches are tools for thinking, not locked boxes."]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "The Toolkits — and the People Behind Them", "lbl": null, "b": ["Today we met the toolkits philosophers use: Metaphysics, Epistemology, Ethics, Social & Political Philosophy, Philosophy of Science.", "Next lesson: we meet the people who built those toolkits across history.", "Four eras: Classical → Medieval → Early Modern → Contemporary.", "Remember the compass: Be Curious First, Not Critical."], "c": []}]}, {"u": 1, "l": 3, "title": "A Brief History of Philosophy", "sub": "Four eras, dozens of names — one long conversation.", "cards": [{"k": "notes", "h": "How to Read This Timeline", "lbl": null, "b": ["Today we're building a mental map of WHO did philosophy, WHEN, WHERE, and on WHAT.", "Four Eras:", "Classical  (~600 BCE – 500 CE)", "Medieval  (~500 – 1400 CE)", "Early Modern  (~1400 – 1800 CE)", "Contemporary  (~1800 CE – today)", "For each era, copy down: the rough dates, where it was centered, its central focus, and 2–3 key names with ONE idea each. You don't need to memorize everything — you need a map you can find your way back to."], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "The Classical Era", "lbl": null, "b": ["~600 BCE – 500 CE   ·   Centered in Ancient Greece, later Rome", "Focus: the first systematic attempts to explain reality, knowledge, and how to live — without relying only on myth.", "Presocratics (Thales, Pythagoras, Heraclitus)", "“What is everything made of?” — first metaphysical questions", "Socrates (470–399 BCE, Athens)", "The Socratic method — relentless questioning to expose what we don't really know", "Plato (428–348 BCE, Athens)", "Theory of Forms; wrote The Republic; taught in the Academy", "Aristotle (384–322 BCE, Athens)", "Logic, virtue ethics, eudaimonia; tutored Alexander the Great"], "c": []}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Questioning Everything", "lbl": null, "b": ["Socrates claimed the wisest thing he knew was that he knew nothing. Is that actually wise, or just frustrating?", "Why might a culture only start asking “what is everything made of?” at a particular point in its history — what conditions might make that question possible?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "The Medieval Era", "lbl": null, "b": ["~500 – 1400 CE   ·   Centered across Europe AND the Islamic world (Persia, North Africa, Spain)", "Focus: reconciling faith and reason — how does philosophy fit with religious belief?", "Augustine of Hippo (354–430 CE, North Africa)", "Blended Christian theology with Platonism", "Boethius (480–524 CE, Rome)", "Wrote The Consolation of Philosophy; gave the definition of “person” from Lesson 1", "Avicenna / Ibn Sina (980–1037, Persia) & Averroes / Ibn Rushd (1126–1198, Spain)", "Major Islamic philosophers who preserved and extended Aristotle's work", "Thomas Aquinas (1225–1274, Italy)", "Synthesized Aristotle with Christian theology in the Summa Theologica"], "c": []}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Faith and Reason", "lbl": null, "b": ["Do you think faith and reason can work together, or are they always in tension? Defend your view.", "Medieval philosophy crossed Christian, Islamic, and Jewish thinkers who all read and responded to each other. Why might that surprise people today?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "The Early Modern Era, Part 1", "lbl": null, "b": ["~1400 – 1800 CE   ·   Centered in Western Europe (France, England, Netherlands, Germany)", "Focus: the rise of science and individual reason — questioning tradition and authority directly.", "René Descartes (1596–1650, France)", "“Cogito, ergo sum”(“I think, therefore I am”); radical doubt as a method (recall Lesson 1's dreaming question!)", "Thomas Hobbes (1588–1679, England)", "Leviathan; life without government is “solitary, poor, nasty, brutish, and short”", "Baruch Spinoza (1632–1677, Netherlands) & Gottfried Leibniz (1646–1716, Germany)", "Rationalist metaphysics — building reality's structure from pure reason"], "c": [{"t": "KEY TERM — CALLBACK", "b": ["Descartes is our Lesson 1 & 2 Rationalist: knowledge built primarily from reason, starting with radical doubt."]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "The Early Modern Era, Part 2", "lbl": null, "b": ["John Locke (1632–1704, England)", "Empiricism — the mind starts as a “blank slate”; natural rights; social contract", "David Hume (1711–1776, Scotland)", "Radical empiricism and skepticism about cause and effect", "Jean-Jacques Rousseau (1712–1778, Geneva/France)", "The Social Contract — “man is born free, and everywhere he is in chains”", "Immanuel Kant (1724–1804, Prussia)", "Tried to combine Rationalism and Empiricism; the Categorical Imperative (recall Lesson 2's Deontology!)"], "c": []}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Reason vs. Experience", "lbl": null, "b": ["Locke vs. Descartes: do you think we're born with any knowledge at all, or does everything come from experience?", "Hobbes and Locke both used “the social contract” but reached different conclusions about government. What might explain that?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "The Contemporary Era", "lbl": null, "b": ["~1800 CE – today   ·   Increasingly global, especially through the 20th & 21st centuries", "Focus: philosophy diversifies — existentialism, ethics, political philosophy, philosophy of mind and technology.", "Søren Kierkegaard (1813–1855, Denmark) & Friedrich Nietzsche (1844–1900, Germany)", "Early existentialism; Nietzsche's critique of traditional morality (“God is dead”)", "Jean-Paul Sartre (1905–1980) & Simone de Beauvoir (1908–1986, France)", "“Existence precedes essence”; de Beauvoir's The Second Sex — a bridge into Lesson 4", "John Rawls (1921–2002, USA) & Alan Turing (1912–1954, England)", "Rawls' veil of ignorance and Turing's test — both already in your Lesson 1 notes!"], "c": []}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Philosophy Right Now", "lbl": null, "b": ["Contemporary philosophy engages directly with AI, technology, and global politics. What do you think the “Big Question” of THIS decade is?", "Nietzsche argued traditional morality needed to be questioned, not just accepted. Is that a curious act or a critical one — or can it be both?"], "c": []}, {"k": "activity", "h": "Timeline Poster Gallery Walk", "lbl": null, "b": ["In groups of 3–4, you'll each take ONE era: Classical, Medieval, Early Modern, or Contemporary.", "On chart paper, create a mini-poster with:", "The era's rough date range and where it was centered", "2–3 key names, with ONE idea each, in your own words", "One sketch, symbol, or visual that represents the era's focus", "Post your poster, then do a gallery walk: visit the other three eras and add one sticky-note question to each."], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "The Full Timeline, at a Glance", "lbl": null, "b": ["Classical (~600 BCE–500 CE): Greece/Rome — first systematic questions about reality, knowledge, and living well. Socrates, Plato, Aristotle.", "Medieval (~500–1400 CE): Europe & the Islamic world — reconciling faith and reason. Augustine, Boethius, Avicenna, Averroes, Aquinas.", "Early Modern (~1400–1800 CE): Western Europe — reason, science, and the individual challenge tradition. Descartes, Locke, Hume, Rousseau, Kant.", "Contemporary (~1800 CE–today): increasingly global — existentialism, justice, mind, and technology. Nietzsche, Sartre, de Beauvoir, Rawls, Turing."], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "One Map, Many Gaps", "lbl": null, "b": ["Notice: almost every name on today's timeline is a European (or European-descended) man.", "That's not because philosophy only happened there — it's because of whose voices got recorded, translated, taught, and remembered.", "Next lesson, we go looking for the philosophers missing from today's map: women, queer, Black, Indigenous, and other voices philosophy has often left out.", "Remember the compass: Be Curious First, Not Critical — including about the map itself."], "c": []}]}, {"u": 1, "l": 4, "title": "Missing Voices in Philosophy", "sub": "Especially about the map itself.", "cards": [{"k": "notes", "h": "The Canon Is a Choice, Not a Fact", "lbl": null, "b": ["“The canon” = the set of thinkers a field treats as its essential, foundational voices.", "A canon is built — by whose writing got copied, translated, published, taught, and preserved. It is not a neutral record of “who was smart.”", "People were excluded from the philosophical canon for many overlapping reasons: denied education, denied publication, deliberately erased, or simply never translated into the languages later scholars read.", "Today doesn't replace the timeline from Lesson 3 — it complicates it. These thinkers were doing philosophy at the same time, often responding to the same questions."], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Hypatia of Alexandria (c. 350–415 CE)", "lbl": "VOICE 1 OF 7", "b": ["Neoplatonist philosopher, mathematician, and astronomer in Alexandria, Egypt — Classical era. One of the only well-documented female philosophers from antiquity.", "Led the Neoplatonist school in Alexandria, teaching philosophy, mathematics, and astronomy to students of many faiths.", "Murdered by a mob in 415 CE amid religious and political conflict — her death is often cited as a symbol of Classical learning's decline."], "c": [{"t": "DISCUSS", "b": ["Why might history preserve so few records of women philosophers from antiquity, even when they clearly existed and taught?", "What does it mean that Hypatia is remembered as much for how she died as for what she taught?"]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Zera Yacob (1599–1692)", "lbl": "VOICE 2 OF 7", "b": ["Ethiopian philosopher who wrote the Hatata (“Inquiry”) — a work of rationalist philosophy, written around the same time Descartes was writing in France.", "Independently developed a method of radical doubt and reasoned inquiry into religion and morality, without appealing to any single religious authority.", "Almost entirely absent from Western philosophy courses until recently, despite being a rigorous, original rationalist thinker."], "c": [{"t": "DISCUSS", "b": ["If Zera Yacob and Descartes developed similar methods independently, what does that suggest about where “rational doubt” comes from?", "Why might a thinker like this have been left out of the “official” history of philosophy for so long?"]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Anton Wilhelm Amo (c. 1703–1759)", "lbl": "VOICE 3 OF 7", "b": ["Born in present-day Ghana; brought to Germany as a child, educated at German universities, and eventually earned a doctorate in philosophy.", "Wrote on the mind-body relationship and human rights; taught philosophy at German universities in the same era as Kant.", "Eventually returned to West Africa; his work was largely excluded from later histories of German Enlightenment philosophy."], "c": [{"t": "DISCUSS", "b": ["Amo was writing sophisticated philosophy in the same country, and around the same time, as Kant. Why might only one of their names end up in the standard textbook?", "What does Amo's story suggest about the difference between “who did philosophy” and “who gets remembered for it”?"]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Mary Wollstonecraft (1759–1797)", "lbl": "VOICE 4 OF 7", "b": ["English writer and philosopher; author of A Vindication of the Rights of Woman (1792), a foundational work of feminist philosophy.", "Argued women appear inferior to men not by nature, but because they are systematically denied equal education.", "Directly engaged Enlightenment thinkers of her own era (the same era as Rousseau and Kant), applying their own logic of reason and rights to challenge them."], "c": [{"t": "DISCUSS", "b": ["Wollstonecraft used Enlightenment ideas about reason and rights to argue FOR women's equality. Why might the original Enlightenment thinkers not have applied their own logic this way?", "Is it more powerful to challenge a system from outside it, or by using its own principles against it?"]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Vine Deloria Jr. (1933–2005)", "lbl": "VOICE 5 OF 7", "b": ["Citizen of the Standing Rock Sioux Tribe; author of Custer Died for Your Sins and God Is Red.", "Critiqued Western philosophy and religion for treating time, land, and knowledge in ways that don't match Indigenous worldviews — e.g. linear time and universal truths vs. place-based, relational knowledge.", "Argued Indigenous philosophies deserve to be studied as philosophy in their own right, not filed away as “folklore” or “religion.”"], "c": [{"t": "DISCUSS", "b": ["Deloria argued that WHERE knowledge comes from (a specific place, a specific community) matters. How is that different from how philosophy has usually been taught in this class so far?", "Can a philosophy be true “for a place” without being true everywhere? Does that make it less philosophical?"]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "W.E.B. Du Bois (1868–1963)", "lbl": "VOICE 6 OF 7", "b": ["American sociologist, historian, and philosopher; the first Black person to earn a PhD from Harvard.", "Introduced “double consciousness” in The Souls of Black Folk (1903) — the experience of seeing yourself both through your own eyes and through the eyes of a society that views you as “other.”", "Connects directly back to Lesson 1's Big Question 1 (What is a person?): personal identity can be shaped by social and political forces, not just abstract philosophy."], "c": [{"t": "DISCUSS", "b": ["How does “double consciousness” complicate the definitions of personhood we discussed in Lesson 1?", "Can you think of any form of “double consciousness” in your own life or community, even if the specifics are different?"]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Michel Foucault (1926–1984)", "lbl": "VOICE 7 OF 7", "b": ["French philosopher and historian; one of the most influential 20th-century philosophers, and openly gay at a time when that carried real personal and professional risk.", "Studied how power operates through institutions (prisons, hospitals, schools) and through what a society treats as “normal” or “deviant” — including around sexuality.", "His History of Sexuality argued that categories like “sexuality” itself are shaped by history and power, not fixed, timeless facts."], "c": [{"t": "DISCUSS", "b": ["Foucault argued that ideas of “normal” and “deviant” change across history and are shaped by power. Can you think of an example of something once considered “deviant” that isn't anymore, or vice versa?", "Does knowing that a category was “constructed” by history change how you feel about it?"]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "What These Voices Have in Common", "lbl": null, "b": ["Each of today's thinkers was doing rigorous philosophy — asking and answering Big Questions — often in the same eras as the “canon” names from Lesson 3.", "Each was excluded from the standard timeline for reasons that had nothing to do with the quality of their thinking.", "Their ideas often extend or challenge the canon rather than simply repeating it — double consciousness, place-based knowledge, and reason applied to rights."], "c": [{"t": "BONUS — A TRADITION, NOT JUST A PERSON", "b": ["Ubuntu, a Southern African philosophy, holds that a person becomes a person through community: “I am because we are.” It's a whole ethical framework outside today's individual spotlights, and worth researching further."]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Be Curious First, Not Critical — Revisited", "lbl": null, "b": ["This mantra has been with us since Lesson 1. Today is exactly where it matters most.", "It would be easy to get purely critical here — angry at exclusion, or dismissive of the canon entirely.", "Curiosity asks a harder, more useful question: what can each of these thinkers teach us, on their own terms, that the standard timeline couldn't?", "Being curious about exclusion doesn't mean excusing it — it means staying interested enough to actually learn from what was left out, instead of just being outraged and moving on."], "c": []}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Curiosity About the Canon", "lbl": null, "b": ["How does staying curious (rather than just critical) change how you engage with a tradition that excluded people like today's thinkers?", "Which of today's seven voices did you connect with most, and why?"], "c": []}]}, {"u": 1, "l": 5, "title": "Philosophical Reasoning", "sub": "How to build an argument — and why a paradox can break one.", "cards": [{"k": "watch", "h": "“The Argument Clinic”", "lbl": null, "b": ["Monty Python's Flying Circus (1972)   ·   ~4 min"], "c": [{"t": "WHILE YOU WATCH — JOT DOWN:", "b": ["What does the customer expect to pay for in this sketch?", "According to the sketch, what's the difference between an “argument” and simple “contradiction”?", "Why is this joke actually making a precise philosophical point?", "Search “Monty Python Argument Clinic” on YouTube."]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Reasoning, Arguments & Contradictions", "lbl": null, "b": ["Reasoning: the process of thinking about something in a rational way, in order to form a conclusion or judgment.", "An Argument: “a connected series of statements intended to establish a proposition.”", "Reasons build toward a conclusion — that's what makes it an argument.", "A Contradiction: simply “the automatic gainsaying of anything the other person says.” (courtesy Monty Python's Argument Clinic)"], "c": [{"t": "KEY DISTINCTION", "b": ["A philosophical argument isn't a fight — it's premises building toward a conclusion. Just saying “No it isn't!” is a contradiction, not an argument."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Argument or Contradiction?", "lbl": null, "b": ["Think of a recent disagreement you had (with a friend, online, anywhere). Was it actually an argument, or mostly contradiction? How do you know?", "Why might people confuse “winning an argument” with “having the last contradiction”?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "The Parts of an Argument", "lbl": null, "b": ["Proposition", "A sentence that expresses facts that can be either true or false.", "Premises", "The propositions in an argument that state facts or provide information supporting the claim being made.", "Conclusion", "The claim being made in an argument.", "Arguments always have exactly ONE conclusion — but the number of premises can vary."], "c": [{"t": "KEY TERM", "b": ["“Valid” describes an argument's STRUCTURE — whether the conclusion follows from the premises — not whether the premises are actually true."]}]}, {"k": "activity", "h": "Label the Argument", "lbl": null, "b": ["With a partner, label the premises and conclusion in each argument below:", "1. “All mammals are warm-blooded. A dolphin is a mammal. Therefore, a dolphin is warm-blooded.”", "2. “It's been sunny every day this week. The forecast says sun tomorrow too. So tomorrow will probably be sunny.”", "For each: How many premises? What's the conclusion? Does the conclusion feel 100% guaranteed, or just probable? (Hang onto that instinct — we'll name it very soon.)"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Russell's Barber Paradox", "lbl": null, "b": ["Bertrand Russell — a 20th-century Analytic philosopher — devised this self-referential paradox.", "The setup:", "In a town, a rule says all men must be clean-shaven. There is a barber who shaves all (and only) the men who do not shave themselves.", "The rules:", "(1) The barber must shave all men who do not shave themselves.", "(2) The barber must NOT shave any man who shaves himself.", "The paradox: who shaves the barber?"], "c": [{"t": "WHY IT MATTERS", "b": ["Russell used this to expose a deep problem in logic and set theory: “the group of all men who shave themselves” can't consistently include or exclude the barber. Self-reference can break a system's own rules."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Who Shaves the Barber?", "lbl": null, "b": ["Try to “solve” the paradox out loud with a partner. Where does your solution break down?", "Can you think of another rule that ends up contradicting itself when applied to itself?"], "c": []}, {"k": "watch", "h": "“How to Argue — Philosophical Reasoning”", "lbl": null, "b": ["Crash Course Philosophy #2 (Hank Green)   ·   ~10 min"], "c": [{"t": "WHILE YOU WATCH — JOT DOWN:", "b": ["How does Hank define a deductively VALID argument?", "What's the difference between an argument being “valid” and being “sound”?", "What example does Hank use to illustrate deductive reasoning?"]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Deductive Arguments", "lbl": null, "b": ["Deductive arguments are meant to be watertight: IF the premises are true, THEN the conclusion MUST be true.", "The classic example:", "1. All men are mortal.", "2. Socrates is a man.", "3. Therefore, Socrates is mortal.", "It is simply not possible for both premises to be true and the conclusion false — that's what makes this argument deductively valid."], "c": [{"t": "KEY TERM", "b": ["Valid = correct structure (the conclusion follows). Sound = valid AND all the premises are actually true."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Building a Valid Argument", "lbl": null, "b": ["Can a deductive argument be VALID but still have a false conclusion? How would that happen?", "Try building your own valid deductive argument using any two true premises."], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Inductive Arguments", "lbl": null, "b": ["Inductive arguments are NOT watertight: they argue that because something has always happened, it's reasonable to believe it will keep happening.", "The classic example:", "1. The sun has not exploded for all its existence.", "2. Therefore, the sun will not explode tomorrow.", "We're not 100% certain the conclusion is true — but based on past outcomes, it's highly probable."], "c": [{"t": "KEY TERM", "b": ["Inductive strength is a matter of degree — arguments can be stronger or weaker, not simply valid or invalid like deductive ones."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Certain vs. Probable", "lbl": null, "b": ["Give one example of an inductive argument from your own life — something you believe because it's “always” been true.", "Why might scientists rely heavily on inductive reasoning, even though it can never be 100% certain?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Coming Up: Logical Fallacies", "lbl": null, "b": ["Formal Fallacies", "Errors in the STRUCTURE of a deductive argument — the premises don't actually support the conclusion the way they're claimed to.", "Informal Fallacies", "Since no inductive argument is ever 100% certain, informal fallacies are about weaknesses in HOW CONVINCING the reasoning is, not a broken structure.", "Next lesson, we'll study specific fallacies in three categories: Relevance, Accent, and Presumption."], "c": []}]}, {"u": 1, "l": 6, "title": "Logical Fallacies", "sub": "Spotting flawed reasoning without just trying to “win.”", "cards": [{"k": "notes", "h": "Recap: Formal vs. Informal Fallacies", "lbl": null, "b": ["Formal Fallacies", "Errors in the STRUCTURE of a deductive argument — the premises don't actually guarantee the conclusion, even if they look like they should.", "Informal Fallacies", "Weaknesses in HOW CONVINCING an argument's reasoning actually is — today's focus, in three categories:", "Relevance — support that isn't actually relevant to the claim", "Ambiguity — flawed due to unclear or shifting meaning", "Presumption — treats an unproven claim as if it were already established"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Relevance Fallacies (1 of 2)", "lbl": null, "b": ["Ad Hominem (Personal Attack)", "Attacking the person's character instead of their argument. “You can't trust John's opinion on climate change; he's not even a scientist.”", "Bandwagon Fallacy", "An argument accepted as true simply because many people believe it. “Everyone is switching to this new diet, so it must be the best way to lose weight!”", "Irrelevant Appeals", "Persuading using information unrelated to the argument. “We should ban video games because my grandmother thinks they are corrupting youth.”", "Moralistic Fallacy", "Assumes what ought to be true must be true. “People shouldn't lie, therefore politicians must be telling the truth.”", "Naturalistic Fallacy", "Assumes what is natural is inherently good or right. “We shouldn't use medicine to treat depression because sadness is a natural human emotion.”"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Relevance Fallacies (2 of 2)", "lbl": null, "b": ["Red Herring", "Introducing an unrelated topic to divert attention. “Why worry about environmental issues when we have bigger problems like crime?”", "Fallacy of Division", "Assuming what's true for the whole must be true for its parts. “The basketball team is the best in the league, so every player must be the best in their position.”", "Gambler's Fallacy", "Assuming past events influence probability in independent events. “I've lost five coin flips in a row, so the next one has to be heads!”", "Genetic Fallacy", "Judging an argument by its origin rather than its content. “We shouldn't believe this study because it was funded by a corporation.”"], "c": []}, {"k": "activity", "h": "Which Relevance Fallacy Is This?", "lbl": null, "b": ["“Don't listen to her opinion on the new tax law — she's not even an economist.”", "“We shouldn't ban the pesticide — my uncle says it's fine, and he's been farming for 40 years.”", "Name the specific fallacy for each, and explain in one sentence why the reasoning fails."], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Ambiguity Fallacies", "lbl": null, "b": ["Equivocation Fallacy", "Using a word with multiple meanings inconsistently. “A feather is light. What is light cannot be dark. Therefore, a feather cannot be dark.”", "Straw Man Fallacy", "Misrepresenting an opponent's argument to make it easier to refute. “Person A: We should have stricter background checks for guns. Person B: So you want to take away everyone's guns?”", "Accent Fallacy", "Shifting the meaning of a sentence by changing the stress or emphasis from one word to another. “I didn’t say she stole the money” vs “I didn’t say she stole the money”."], "c": [{"t": "NOTE", "b": ["These three Ambiguity fallacies work the same way: they exploit unclear or shifting language, rather than unclear logic, to seem more convincing than they are."]}]}, {"k": "activity", "h": "Which Ambiguity Fallacy Is This?", "lbl": null, "b": ["“The sign said 'fine for parking here,' so I thought it was fine to park here.”", "“Senator Lee wants to cut the defense budget by 5%. He must want to leave our country totally defenseless.”", "Name the specific fallacy for each, and explain in one sentence why the reasoning fails."], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Presumption Fallacies (1 of 3)", "lbl": null, "b": ["Arguing from Ignorance", "Assuming something is true because it hasn't been proven false. “No one has ever proven that ghosts don't exist, so they must be real!”", "Begging the Question / Circular Reasoning", "The conclusion is assumed within the argument's own premises. “God exists because the Bible says so, and the Bible is true because it's the word of God.”", "Complex Question Fallacy", "A question structured to assume something unproven. “Have you stopped cheating on your exams?”", "Cum Hoc Fallacy", "Assuming two things occurring together must be causally related. “People who exercise more tend to be happier, so exercise must directly cause happiness.”"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Presumption Fallacies (2 of 3)", "lbl": null, "b": ["False Dilemma / Bifurcation Fallacy", "Presenting only two options when more exist. “You're either with us or against us!”", "Hasty Generalization Fallacy", "Drawing a conclusion from too small a sample size. “I met two rude Canadians, so all Canadians must be rude.”", "“No True Scotsman” Fallacy", "Reinterpreting evidence to exclude counterexamples. “No real artist uses digital tools.” “But Picasso used experimental techniques.” “Well, no TRUE artist would do that!”", "Post Hoc Fallacy", "Assuming that because one event followed another, the first caused the second. “I wore my lucky socks, and we won the game. My socks must be lucky!”"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Presumption Fallacies (3 of 3)", "lbl": null, "b": ["Slippery Slope Fallacy", "Assuming one action will inevitably lead to extreme consequences. “If we allow self-driving cars, soon robots will take over the world!”", "Sweeping Generalization Fallacy", "Applying a general rule too broadly. “All teenagers are irresponsible, so we shouldn't let them drive.”", "Subjectivist Fallacy", "Dismissing an argument because of personal belief. “That might be true for you, but I don't believe in evolution, so it's false for me.”", "Tu Quoque Fallacy", "Deflecting criticism by pointing out hypocrisy. “You say smoking is bad, but you used to smoke, so I don't have to listen to you.”"], "c": []}, {"k": "activity", "h": "Which Presumption Fallacy Is This?", "lbl": null, "b": ["“You either support the new policy completely, or you don't care about students at all.”", "“I met two people from that city and they were both rude, so people there must be rude.”", "Name the specific fallacy for each, and explain in one sentence why the reasoning fails."], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Why This Actually Matters", "lbl": null, "b": ["Fallacies aren't just an academic exercise — they show up constantly in ads, political speech, headlines, and social media, sometimes by accident and sometimes on purpose.", "Important nuance: spotting a fallacy doesn't prove the CONCLUSION is false — it means the ARGUMENT given for it doesn't actually work.", "That's a curious move, not just a critical one: you're not dismissing the person, you're examining the reasoning."], "c": [{"t": "CALLBACK", "b": ["Be Curious First, Not Critical: the goal of spotting fallacies isn't to “win” arguments or shut people down — it's to think more clearly, together."]}]}, {"k": "activity", "h": "Find Your Own Fallacy", "lbl": null, "b": ["Individually or in pairs, find ONE real example of a logical fallacy — an ad, a headline, a social media post, a speech, or something you've heard recently.", "Identify:", "What is the specific fallacy?", "Which category does it belong to — Relevance, Ambiguity, or Presumption?", "Why does it fail to actually support its conclusion?", "Be ready to share (a screenshot, a quote, or just a description) at the start of next class."], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Unit 1, Start to Finish", "lbl": null, "b": ["Lesson 1 — Foundations: what is philosophy, and the Big Six (+1) Questions.", "Lesson 2 — The Branches: Metaphysics, Epistemology, Ethics, and a light touch of two more.", "Lesson 3 — History: four eras, dozens of names, one long conversation.", "Lesson 4 — Missing Voices: whose philosophy got left off the standard map.", "Lessons 5–6 — Reasoning: how arguments are built, and how reasoning goes wrong.", "All six lessons feed your Unit 1 test — review all six worksheets and slide decks."], "c": [{"t": "THE THROUGH-LINE", "b": ["“Be Curious First, Not Critical” ran through every single lesson. Keep it — it doesn't expire at the end of the unit."]}]}]}, {"u": 2, "l": 1, "title": "What Is Knowledge?", "sub": "The nature and limits of knowing.", "cards": [{"k": "notes", "h": "What Is Knowledge?", "lbl": null, "b": ["Epistemology: the study of knowledge — what it is, and how (or whether) we can have it.", "Central questions for this whole unit:", "What is knowledge? What does it mean when we say someone “knows” something?", "Recall from Unit 1: the traditional definition of knowledge is justified true belief (JTB)."], "c": [{"t": "THINK ABOUT IT", "b": ["“Knowing that” (Paris is the capital of France) is different from “knowing how” (riding a bike). This unit mostly explores knowing-THAT."]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "The Extent of Human Knowledge", "lbl": null, "b": ["How do we acquire knowledge in the first place? (We'll dig into this in Lesson 2.)", "How much do we — or can we — actually know?", "Is it possible we don't know nearly as much as we think we do?", "Are some things permanently unknowable — not just “not yet known,” but unknowable in principle?"], "c": [{"t": "OLD IDEA, NEW PHRASE", "b": ["Socrates: “I know that I know nothing.” Modern version: “known unknowns vs. unknown unknowns.” Same epistemic humility, 2,400 years apart."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "The Limits of Knowing", "lbl": null, "b": ["Name one thing you're completely certain you know. Now: could you be wrong?", "Give an example of something that might be unknowable to humans in principle — not just undiscovered yet."], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Recap: Justified True Belief", "lbl": null, "b": ["Knowledge = Justified True Belief (JTB)", "A belief that is (1) true, (2) actually believed, and (3) justified.", "This definition traces back to Plato and stood as the standard account of knowledge for over 2,000 years.", "It seems intuitive... but in 1963, one three-page philosophy paper broke it."], "c": [{"t": "KEY TERM", "b": ["Justification: the reasons or evidence that make a belief reasonable to hold — not just true by luck."]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "The Gettier Problem", "lbl": null, "b": ["Edmund Gettier (1963) — a short paper, “Is Justified True Belief Knowledge?”, changed epistemology permanently.", "Gettier cases: situations where someone has a justified, true belief — but we still wouldn't say they “know” it, because the truth is a matter of luck or coincidence.", "Since 1963, philosophers have debated: what needs to be added to JTB to capture real knowledge?"], "c": [{"t": "WHY IT MATTERS", "b": ["If JTB isn't sufficient, every time you say “I know X,” you're relying on a definition philosophers still haven't fully agreed on."]}]}, {"k": "watch", "h": "“The Meaning of Knowledge”", "lbl": null, "b": ["Crash Course Philosophy #7 (Hank Green)   ·   ~10 min"], "c": [{"t": "WHILE YOU WATCH — JOT DOWN:", "b": ["What are the three traditional conditions for knowledge, according to Hank?", "In Hank's example(s), what makes a belief justified and true, but NOT knowledge?", "What does Gettier's challenge suggest about how confident we should be in “obvious” definitions?"]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "A Gettier Case, Worked Through", "lbl": null, "b": ["Bob believes his friend Alice drives a Ford, because he saw her driving one yesterday — a true belief, justified by good evidence.", "Unknown to Bob, Alice was actually driving a RENTAL that day. BUT — by total coincidence, Alice does own a Ford, parked at home.", "Bob's belief is justified AND true... but it doesn't feel like knowledge. His justification is disconnected from the reason the belief happens to be true."], "c": [{"t": "KEY TERM", "b": ["Epistemic luck: when a belief turns out true by accident, not because the justification actually tracked the truth."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Building Your Own Gettier Case", "lbl": null, "b": ["Try to build your own mini Gettier case: a justified, true belief that still doesn't feel like “knowledge.” (Hint: base it on a coincidence.)", "Does the existence of Gettier cases mean we should give up trying to define knowledge? Why or why not?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Skepticism, Revisited", "lbl": null, "b": ["Recall from Unit 1: Descartes' method of radical doubt — “How do I know I'm not dreaming?”", "Global skepticism: the view that we can't have certain knowledge of the external world at all.", "Descartes didn't stay a skeptic — he used doubt as a METHOD, searching for one thing he could be certain of (“I think, therefore I am”)."], "c": [{"t": "KEY TERM", "b": ["Cartesian doubt: doubting everything that can possibly be doubted, to find out what (if anything) survives."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Modern Skepticism", "lbl": null, "b": ["AI-generated images, video, and text (“deepfakes”) make it harder to trust what we see and hear. Is this a NEW epistemological problem, or an old one (like Descartes' doubt) in new clothes?", "If you can never be 100% certain of anything, does that mean you should stop believing anything?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "The Questions Driving This Unit", "lbl": null, "b": ["Today we didn't answer “what is knowledge?” — we complicated it. That's the point of epistemology.", "Big questions ahead this unit:", "Where does knowledge come from? How do we justify belief? What is truth? Whose truth counts?", "Remember the compass: Be Curious First, Not Critical — especially when a 2,000-year-old definition gets challenged in three pages."], "c": []}]}, {"u": 2, "l": 2, "title": "Where Does Knowledge Come From?", "sub": "Rationalism vs. Empiricism.", "cards": [{"k": "notes", "h": "A Priori vs. A Posteriori", "lbl": null, "b": ["A Priori", "Knowledge or justification that is INDEPENDENT of experience (e.g. math, logic).", "A Posteriori", "Knowledge based on experience — observational knowledge can only be gained this way.", "Recall Unit 1: you've already used these terms once. Today we build a full theory around them."], "c": [{"t": "KEY TERM", "b": ["Deduction: the inference of particular instances by reference to a general law or principle — a classic a priori reasoning tool."]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Rationalism", "lbl": null, "b": ["Rationalism: the view that at least some human knowledge is gained a priori — prior to experience.", "René Descartes (recall!): “I think, therefore I am” — certainty built from reason alone.", "Innate: the doctrine that the mind is born with ideas/knowledge already present.", "Intuition: a thing one knows or considers likely from instinctive feeling rather than conscious reasoning."], "c": [{"t": "KEY FIGURES", "b": ["Descartes, Spinoza, and Leibniz form the classic 17th-century “Continental Rationalists.” Spinoza described three kinds of knowledge: opinion/imagination (least reliable), reason, and intuitive knowledge (most certain)."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Is Anything Truly Innate?", "lbl": null, "b": ["Can you think of anything you seem to “just know” without ever being taught it?", "Newborns share certain instincts (e.g. fear of falling). Does that count as “innate knowledge,” or just biology?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Leibniz vs. Locke: The Marble Debate", "lbl": null, "b": ["Gottfried Leibniz (1646–1716, Germany) defended innate ideas directly against Locke's rejection of them.", "Leibniz's marble metaphor: ideas are innate the way veins are already present in a block of marble, waiting to be revealed by the sculptor's work — not fully formed thoughts, but dispositions or tendencies we're born with.", "Leibniz's famous amendment to the empiricist slogan: “There is nothing in the intellect that was not first in the senses — except the intellect itself.”"], "c": [{"t": "WHY IT MATTERS", "b": ["This debate isn't settled history — it's the direct ancestor of today's “nature vs. nurture” questions in psychology and linguistics."]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Empiricism", "lbl": null, "b": ["Empiricism: the view that knowledge comes primarily through sense-experience.", "Sense-Perception: sight, smell, hearing, taste, and touch — the faculties by which the body receives external stimuli.", "John Locke: the mind begins as a “tabula rasa” — a blank slate — filled in only by experience.", "Locke directly rejected Descartes' innate ideas."], "c": [{"t": "KEY TERM", "b": ["Tabula Rasa (Latin: “blank slate”) — Locke's term for the mind at birth, before any experience has written on it."]}]}, {"k": "watch", "h": "“Locke, Berkeley, & Empiricism”", "lbl": null, "b": ["Crash Course Philosophy #6 (Hank Green)   ·   ~10 min"], "c": [{"t": "WHILE YOU WATCH — JOT DOWN:", "b": ["What are Locke's “primary” and “secondary” qualities, and why does the distinction matter?", "Why does Berkeley think Locke's distinction “doesn't work”?", "What are we left with, according to Berkeley, if we follow empiricism all the way?"]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Rationalism vs. Empiricism", "lbl": null, "b": ["Which camp feels more intuitively correct to you: Rationalism or Empiricism? Why?", "Locke rejected innate ideas partly because babies and uneducated people don't display universal ideas (like knowledge of God). Is that a fair test?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Kant's Synthesis", "lbl": null, "b": ["Recall Unit 1: Immanuel Kant tried to combine Rationalism and Empiricism.", "Kant's view: the senses give us raw experience, but the MIND actively structures that experience using built-in categories (like space, time, and causation).", "Neither pure reason nor pure experience alone is enough — knowledge requires both."], "c": [{"t": "KEY QUOTE (paraphrased)", "b": ["Kant: “Thoughts without content are empty; intuitions without concepts are blind.”"]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Modern Connection: Is Anything Really “Built In”?", "lbl": null, "b": ["Linguist Noam Chomsky argued humans have an innate “universal grammar” — a built-in capacity for language that pure experience alone couldn't fully explain.", "This modern, rationalist-style claim is still debated today in cognitive science and linguistics.", "The “nature vs. nurture” debate in psychology asks a strikingly similar epistemological question — just about traits and behaviors instead of ideas."], "c": []}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Nature vs. Nurture, Today", "lbl": null, "b": ["If Chomsky is right that language ability is innate, does that support Rationalism? Why might an Empiricist push back?", "Where do you personally land on “nature vs. nurture” for something like personality or morality?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Mapping the Sources of Knowledge", "lbl": null, "b": ["Rationalism: reason, deduction, intuition, innate ideas — a priori.", "Empiricism: sense-perception, observation, experiment — a posteriori.", "Kant: both, working together.", "Next lesson: even once we know WHERE knowledge comes from, we still need to ask HOW SURE we can be that it's justified."], "c": []}]}, {"u": 2, "l": 3, "title": "How Do We Justify Belief?", "sub": "Certainty, method, and the evidence we trust.", "cards": [{"k": "notes", "h": "Certainty: Infallibilism vs. Fallibilism", "lbl": null, "b": ["Infallibilism", "Knowledge is, by definition, a true belief which CANNOT be rationally doubted. The justification must show the belief is incapable of being untrue.", "Fallibilism", "No belief can be rationally supported or justified in a conclusive way. There always remains a POSSIBLE doubt.", "Two opposing answers to: how sure do we need to be, to count as knowing?"], "c": [{"t": "KEY TENSION", "b": ["If Infallibilism is true, we may know almost nothing. If Fallibilism is true, “knowledge” might just mean “very well-supported belief.”"]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Evidentialism & Occam's Razor", "lbl": null, "b": ["Evidentialism", "A thesis about epistemic justification: what does it take for someone to believe justifiably or reasonably? Roughly — your belief is justified if and only if it fits your evidence.", "Occam's Razor", "Explanations of unknown phenomena are sought first in terms of known quantities — prefer the SIMPLEST explanation that fits the evidence."], "c": [{"t": "KEY FIGURE", "b": ["William of Ockham (c. 1287–1347), an English Medieval friar and philosopher — recall the Medieval era from Unit 1! His “razor” is still cited in science and philosophy today."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "How Sure Is Sure Enough?", "lbl": null, "b": ["Do you lean more Infallibilist or Fallibilist? Does it depend on the topic — math vs. history vs. your own feelings?", "Give an example of Occam's Razor at work: a time you (or someone) picked the simplest explanation over a more complicated one."], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Causation & Law of Nature", "lbl": null, "b": ["Causation: the relationship between cause and effect.", "Law of Nature: a regularly occurring, apparently inevitable phenomenon observable in the natural world.", "David Hume's problem: we never directly OBSERVE causation — we only observe one event followed by another, over and over. We ASSUME the connection.", "This is the problem of induction (recall Unit 1, Lesson 5): the sun rising every day doesn't guarantee it will tomorrow."], "c": [{"t": "KEY QUESTION", "b": ["If we can't prove causation — only observe correlation and repetition — how much can science actually promise us?"]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "The Scientific Method & Probability", "lbl": null, "b": ["Scientific Method: relevant data are gathered, a hypothesis is formulated from the data, and the hypothesis is empirically tested.", "Recall Unit 1: Karl Popper argued a theory only counts as scientific if it's falsifiable.", "Probability Theory: the extent to which an event is likely to occur — science often can't promise certainty, only probability."], "c": [{"t": "KEY TERM", "b": ["Falsifiability (Popper): it must be POSSIBLE, in principle, to prove a theory wrong for it to count as scientific."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Can Science Ever Be Certain?", "lbl": null, "b": ["If scientific theories are always technically falsifiable, can science ever give us TRUE knowledge — or just “the best current explanation”?", "Give an example of something once treated as a scientific “law” that was later revised or overturned."], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Perception & Reliability", "lbl": null, "b": ["Much of what we believe comes through perception — sight, hearing, touch. But how RELIABLE is perception, really?", "Reliabilism: a belief is justified if it was produced by a reliable cognitive process — one that tends to produce true beliefs.", "Classic problems: optical illusions, eyewitness misidentification, and false memories all show that perception can FEEL completely certain and still be wrong."], "c": [{"t": "KEY STAT", "b": ["Eyewitness misidentification has been a contributing factor in the majority of U.S. wrongful convictions later overturned by DNA evidence (Innocence Project research) — a very real-world stake for epistemology."]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Types of Knowledge Claims", "lbl": null, "b": ["Factual Knowledge", "Information that is verifiable and agreed upon by almost everyone.", "Statistical Inferences", "Interpretation drawn from an accumulation of facts.", "Informed Opinion", "Opinion developed through research and/or expertise on the claim.", "Personal Testimony", "Personal experience related by a knowledgeable party."], "c": [{"t": "KEY SKILL", "b": ["Not all “evidence” is equally strong. Sorting a claim into one of these four categories is a core critical thinking skill — on and off social media."]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "When Justification Goes Wrong", "lbl": null, "b": ["Groupthink", "Approaching problems as matters best dealt with by consensus of a group, rather than by individuals acting independently.", "Fatalism", "The belief that all events are predetermined and therefore inevitable.", "Groupthink can make a belief FEEL justified (everyone agrees!) without it actually being well-supported by evidence.", "Fatalism can make people stop seeking justification altogether — “what's the point, it was always going to happen.”"], "c": [{"t": "WHY IT MATTERS", "b": ["“Groupthink” (Irving Janis, 1972) was coined to explain real disasters caused by group decision-making that overrode individual doubts."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Groupthink in the Wild", "lbl": null, "b": ["Where have you seen groupthink happen — in a friend group, online, in the news? What made it hard for someone to disagree?", "How is an online “echo chamber” a modern, technological version of groupthink?"], "c": []}, {"k": "activity", "h": "The High Cost of Being Wrong", "lbl": null, "b": ["Epistemology in the Real World", "In groups, you'll examine ONE real case where a justified-feeling belief turned out to be false — and consider what it cost.", "Case options: a wrongful conviction built on eyewitness misidentification; the Challenger space shuttle disaster (engineers' warnings overridden); a once-“settled” medical practice later reversed; a financial bubble driven by collective overconfidence.", "For your case, identify:", "What made the belief FEEL justified at the time?", "What actually went wrong — unreliable perception, groupthink, a flawed method, or something else from today's notes?", "What was the real-world cost of being wrong?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Today's Justification Toolkit", "lbl": null, "b": ["How certain we need to be: Infallibilism vs. Fallibilism.", "What counts as good evidence: Evidentialism, Occam's Razor, reliable perception, and the four types of knowledge claims.", "How justification can go wrong: Groupthink and Fatalism — sometimes with very real costs.", "None of these tools tell us what's TRUE — only how confidently and carefully we should believe something. Next lesson, we tackle truth itself."], "c": []}]}, {"u": 2, "l": 4, "title": "What Is Truth?", "sub": "Correspondence, coherence, and pragmatism.", "cards": [{"k": "notes", "h": "The Correspondence Theory of Truth", "lbl": null, "b": ["Correspondence: the truth or falsity of a statement is determined only by how it relates to the world — whether it accurately describes, or corresponds, with that world.", "This is as close as we come to “objective” knowledge — knowledge of things “as they are in themselves.”", "Traces back to Aristotle; defended by modern philosophers like Bertrand Russell."], "c": [{"t": "KEY IDEA", "b": ["“The cat is on the mat” is true IF AND ONLY IF the cat is actually on the mat. Truth = matching reality."]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "The Coherence Theory of Truth", "lbl": null, "b": ["Coherence: the truth of a belief depends on the degree to which it coheres — or aligns — with all other beliefs in a system of beliefs.", "Example: a popular set of beliefs — if everyone else agrees something is true, then it must be so.", "Notice: this can shade into groupthink from Lesson 3, if we're not careful."], "c": [{"t": "KEY WEAKNESS", "b": ["A perfectly coherent, internally consistent belief system could still be entirely disconnected from reality — like a well-organized conspiracy theory."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Matching Reality vs. Matching Beliefs", "lbl": null, "b": ["Give an example where a belief could be coherent (fits with everything else you believe) but NOT actually true.", "Which theory feels more useful for everyday life: checking against reality, or checking against consistency?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Pragmatism", "lbl": null, "b": ["Pragmatism: you can't conceive of the truth of a belief without also conceiving of how, if true, that belief matters in the world. Truth is discovered only through interaction with the world.", "If we say an apple is red, we can't say that without understanding what “redness” is in relation to other red things — a balloon, a clown's nose."], "c": [{"t": "KEY FIGURE", "b": ["William James (1842–1910) — pragmatism developed alongside Charles Sanders Peirce and John Dewey as a distinctly American philosophical movement."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Does Truth Have to “Work”?", "lbl": null, "b": ["Give an example of a belief that “works” for someone — helps them function or feel better — but might not be true. Is that a problem for Pragmatism?", "Can you think of something that's true but doesn't seem to “work” or matter practically at all?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "The Metaphysical Backdrop: Idealism vs. Materialism", "lbl": null, "b": ["Idealism: the claim that everything that exists either IS a mind, or depends on a mind for its existence.", "Materialism: the claim that matter exists independent of human experience.", "Recall Lesson 2 and Crash Course #6: George Berkeley pushed empiricism to its idealist limit.", "These theories matter for truth: what your theory is “matching” or “cohering with” depends on what you think REALLY exists."], "c": [{"t": "KEY FIGURE", "b": ["George Berkeley (recall Lesson 2!) is the classic idealist: no mind observing it, no world — “to be is to be perceived.”"]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Two More Positions on Reality", "lbl": null, "b": ["Subjective / Empirical Idealism", "The monistic metaphysical doctrine that only minds and mental contents exist.", "Transcendental Realism", "A philosophy of science that views things in terms of how they actually ARE, rather than how they appear to the viewer."], "c": [{"t": "NOTE", "b": ["These aren't just abstract labels — they're competing answers to “what is truth ultimately ABOUT?”"]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Modern Connection: Truth in a “Post-Truth” Era", "lbl": null, "b": ["“Post-truth” politics, fake news, and deepfakes have made shared truth harder to agree on.", "Each theory explains this crisis differently:", "Correspondence theorists worry reality itself is being obscured.", "Coherence theorists worry about competing, internally consistent “belief bubbles.”", "Pragmatists ask what actually happens when people act on false information."], "c": []}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Truth in a Post-Truth World", "lbl": null, "b": ["Which theory of truth best explains why two people can look at the same news story and reach opposite conclusions about whether it's true?", "Do you think objective truth exists, even if we can't always access it perfectly? Defend your position."], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Mapping the Theories of Truth", "lbl": null, "b": ["Correspondence: truth = matches reality.", "Coherence: truth = fits your belief system.", "Pragmatism: truth = what works in practice.", "Idealism vs. Materialism: what's actually real, for truth to match or cohere with?", "Next lesson: even if we agree on a theory of truth, do different people or cultures have access to different truths?"], "c": []}]}, {"u": 2, "l": 5, "title": "Whose Truth?", "sub": "Relativism, perspective, and postmodernism.", "cards": [{"k": "notes", "h": "Ideologies & the Seeds of Relativism", "lbl": null, "b": ["Ideology: a set of beliefs or principles that shapes how a person or group interprets the world.", "So far this unit, we've mostly asked: what IS truth? Today we ask a harder question: is truth the SAME for everyone?", "Relativism, broadly: the view that truth, morality, or knowledge is not absolute, but relative to a particular standpoint — a person, culture, or era."], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Moral & Cultural Relativism", "lbl": null, "b": ["Moral Relativism", "Moral judgments (good/bad, right/wrong) should only be assessed relative to a particular cultural standpoint — neither side is simply “wrong,” they are relative.", "Cultural Relativism", "Regarding the beliefs, values, and practices of a culture from the viewpoint of that culture itself. All cultures are worthy in their own right."], "c": [{"t": "KEY TENSION", "b": ["If moral relativism is fully true, can we ever call a historical atrocity “wrong” — or only “wrong by our standards”?"]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Are All Moral Views Equally Valid?", "lbl": null, "b": ["Can you think of a moral practice that differs sharply between cultures? Is one side simply right and the other wrong, or is it genuinely relative?", "Does cultural relativism make it harder or easier to criticize injustice happening in another culture?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Cognitive & Aesthetic Relativism", "lbl": null, "b": ["Cognitive Relativism", "Asserts the relativity of TRUTH itself. Truth depends on the standpoint from which it's judged — individual standpoint = subjectivism; cultural standpoint = cultural relativism.", "Aesthetic Relativism", "Judgments of beauty are relative to individuals and/or cultures — there are no universal criteria of beauty."], "c": [{"t": "KEY TERM", "b": ["Subjectivism: the most individual-level form of relativism — truth or value depends on YOUR standpoint alone."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Is Beauty Ever Objective?", "lbl": null, "b": ["Is there anything you consider objectively beautiful — true for everyone, everywhere? Or is beauty always relative?", "If aesthetic relativism is true, can there be “bad art,” or just “art some people don't personally enjoy”?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Nietzsche's Perspectivism", "lbl": null, "b": ["Recall Unit 1, Lesson 3: Friedrich Nietzsche critiqued traditional, “absolute” morality.", "Perspectivism: all knowledge and truth-claims come from some particular perspective — there is no purely neutral “view from nowhere.”", "This doesn't mean “anything goes” — some perspectives can still be richer, more honest, or more life-affirming than others, in Nietzsche's view."], "c": [{"t": "KEY QUOTE (paraphrased)", "b": ["Nietzsche: there are no facts, only interpretations — and even that is an interpretation."]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Postmodern Philosophy", "lbl": null, "b": ["Postmodern Philosophy: a late 20th-century movement of critical thought focused on how we PERCEIVE and DESCRIBE the world, rather than assuming one objective account of it.", "Jean-François Lyotard famously defined postmodernism as “incredulity toward metanarratives” — skepticism toward big, universal stories that claim to explain everything.", "Postmodernism represents a shift in Western Enlightenment values toward a more inclusive, less-stable worldview."], "c": [{"t": "KEY FIGURE", "b": ["Jean-François Lyotard (1924–1998), French philosopher, The Postmodern Condition (1979)."]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Situatedness & Feminist Standpoint Theory", "lbl": null, "b": ["Situatedness", "The necessity of viewing or understanding life and existence from the “inside” — a particular geographical, socio-historical, or cultural context.", "Feminist Standpoint Theory", "Recognizes that knowledge is “socially situated.” Proposes that marginalized experiences should be a starting point and worldview — not treated as secondary to a default perspective."], "c": [{"t": "KEY FIGURE", "b": ["Sandra Harding, a leading feminist philosopher of science, helped develop standpoint theory's application to epistemology and science."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Does Where You Stand Change What You Know?", "lbl": null, "b": ["Recall Unit 1, Lesson 4: can someone from a Eurocentric tradition truly experience the worldview of someone from a marginalized culture? What's your answer now?", "If knowledge is “situated,” does that make some knowledge MORE reliable (closer to lived reality), or just DIFFERENT?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Modern Connection: Filter Bubbles & “My Truth”", "lbl": null, "b": ["Modern life increasingly involves personalized algorithms showing each of us different information — a technological, everyday version of relativism.", "The popular phrase “my truth” suggests personal experience itself can be a kind of truth — echoing subjectivism and standpoint theory."], "c": []}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "“My Truth” in Everyday Language", "lbl": null, "b": ["When someone says “that's just my truth,” what philosophical position from today are they (maybe unknowingly) taking?", "Is there a risk in using “truth” so personally? What might get lost?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Perspective, Mapped", "lbl": null, "b": ["Today we asked: is truth relative to person, culture, or perspective — or is there something more universal underneath?", "The relativism cluster (Moral, Cultural, Cognitive, Aesthetic) asks the same underlying question across different DOMAINS.", "Postmodernism and standpoint theory: knowledge is always situated somewhere — the question is what to do with that fact."], "c": [{"t": "CALLBACK", "b": ["Be Curious First, Not Critical: relativism doesn't mean “nothing is better than anything else.” It means taking perspective seriously before judging."]}]}]}, {"u": 2, "l": 6, "title": "Decolonizing Philosophy", "sub": "Whose knowledge counts — and who decides?", "cards": [{"k": "notes", "h": "Colonization & Epistemology", "lbl": null, "b": ["Colonization: some form of invasion, dispossession, and subjugation of a people. The invasion need not be military — it can begin as a series of geographical encroachments.", "Colonization isn't only political and territorial — it's also EPISTEMOLOGICAL: whose knowledge counts as “real,” whose language can express truth, whose institutions get to certify what's “known.”"], "c": [{"t": "KEY IDEA", "b": ["Recall Unit 1, Lesson 4: “the canon is a choice, not a fact.” Colonization is one of the biggest reasons that choice got made the way it did."]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Postcolonial Theory", "lbl": null, "b": ["Postcolonial Theory: addresses identity, gender, race, and ethnicity as a way to challenge Western ideology — a critical philosophy that critiques the Western intellectual tradition.", "Edward Said (1935–2003): a foundational postcolonial theorist. His book Orientalism (1978) argued that Western scholarship often constructed a distorted, exoticized image of “the East” that helped justify colonial power."], "c": [{"t": "KEY FIGURE", "b": ["Said argued that even seemingly neutral academic knowledge can encode power relationships — echoing Foucault's power/knowledge from Lesson 5 and Unit 1!"]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Whose Lens?", "lbl": null, "b": ["Can you think of an example — in media, textbooks, or a class — where one group's perspective was presented as the “neutral” or “default” one?", "Why might it matter who WRITES the history, science, or philosophy that gets taught?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Decolonization", "lbl": null, "b": ["Decolonization: traditionally, getting rid of colonization or freeing a country from dependence on another. But it's more — a long-term process of divesting colonial power bureaucratically, culturally, linguistically, and psychologically.", "It also means decolonizing MINDS: shifting how colonized peoples view themselves, their culture, and their worldview, away from an imposed lens.", "Linda Tuhiwai Smith (Māori scholar), Decolonizing Methodologies (1999): argued research itself has been a tool of colonization, and called for research led by and accountable to Indigenous communities.", "Frantz Fanon (Martinique/Algeria) explored the psychological effects of colonization on identity and consciousness."], "c": [{"t": "WHY IT MATTERS", "b": ["Decolonization isn't just about the past — it's an active, ongoing question about who gets to produce knowledge today."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Decolonizing the Mind", "lbl": null, "b": ["What might it look like to “decolonize” a subject you study — history, science, literature, or philosophy itself?", "Is it possible to fully separate knowledge from the power of the people who produced or funded it?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Indigenous Knowledge Systems", "lbl": null, "b": ["Recall Unit 1, Lesson 4: Vine Deloria Jr. argued Indigenous knowledge deserves study as philosophy, not filing away as “folklore.”", "Indigenous knowledge systems often emphasize RELATIONAL, place-based knowledge — knowing something is inseparable from your relationship to land, community, and generational memory.", "This directly challenges the common Western-canon assumption that the BEST knowledge is abstract, universal, and detached from any particular place or people."], "c": [{"t": "KEY CONNECTION", "b": ["Recall Ubuntu philosophy from Unit 1: “I am because we are.” Many non-Western epistemologies treat knowing as inherently communal, not just individual."]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Full Circle: The Missing Voices of Epistemology", "lbl": null, "b": ["Recall Unit 1, Lesson 4: Zera Yacob (Ethiopia) developed rigorous rational doubt independently of Descartes.", "Anton Wilhelm Amo (Ghana/Germany) wrote on the mind-body relationship in Kant's own era.", "W.E.B. Du Bois described “double consciousness” as a form of situated knowledge — exactly the concept from Lesson 5.", "None of these thinkers lacked rigor. What they lacked was a canon willing to preserve, translate, and teach them."], "c": []}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Decolonizing This Course", "lbl": null, "b": ["Looking back at this whole unit, whose voices did we spend the MOST time on? Whose did we spend the least?", "What's one epistemological question from this unit you'd like to explore further from a non-Western perspective?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "The Whole Epistemology Toolkit", "lbl": null, "b": ["Lesson 1: What is knowledge? (JTB, Gettier, skepticism)", "Lesson 2: Where does it come from? (Rationalism, Empiricism, Kant)", "Lesson 3: How do we justify it? (Certainty, evidence, reliability, groupthink)", "Lesson 4: What is truth? (Correspondence, Coherence, Pragmatism)", "Lesson 5: Whose truth? (Relativism, postmodernism, standpoint theory)", "Lesson 6: Whose knowledge counts? (Colonization, decolonization)"], "c": [{"t": "THE THROUGH-LINE", "b": ["Be Curious First, Not Critical — especially when the answer to “who's right?” turns out to be more complicated than a single philosopher's word."]}]}]}, {"u": 3, "l": 1, "title": "What Is Metaphysics?", "sub": "The study of being — beyond the physical.", "cards": [{"k": "notes", "h": "Defining Metaphysics", "lbl": null, "b": ["Metaphysics: from Greek meta (beyond) + physika (nature/physics) — literally “beyond the physical.” Recall Unit 1, Lesson 2.", "Central question: what is real, and what is the fundamental nature of reality?", "Metaphysics asks about things not directly observable through the five senses or measurable by science alone: existence itself, identity, causation, possibility, time."], "c": [{"t": "THINK ABOUT IT", "b": ["Physics asks HOW the universe behaves. Metaphysics asks WHAT the universe — and everything in it — fundamentally IS."]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Ontology: The Study of Being", "lbl": null, "b": ["Ontology: the sub-branch of metaphysics concerned with what exists, and the basic categories of being.", "Central ontological question: what KINDS of things exist? Physical objects? Numbers? Minds? Possibilities? Fictional characters?", "Ontological commitment: to believe a theory is true is to be committed to the existence of whatever that theory says exists."], "c": [{"t": "KEY TERM", "b": ["“Ontology” comes from Greek on (being) + logos (study of)."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "What Exists?", "lbl": null, "b": ["Does the number 7 exist? Does it exist the same way a rock exists?", "Does “justice” exist? If so, where and how?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Parmenides: Being Is, Non-Being Is Not", "lbl": null, "b": ["Parmenides (c. 515 BCE, Classical era — recall the Presocratics from Unit 1!) argued that “what is, is; what is not, cannot be.”", "From this, he concluded that change and plurality are illusions — reality must be a single, unchanging, eternal whole.", "His reasoning: if something changes, it must go from what it IS to what it IS NOT — but “what is not” cannot exist, so change is logically impossible."], "c": [{"t": "KEY IDEA", "b": ["Parmenides didn't deny that change APPEARS to happen — he denied it could be logically real."]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Heraclitus: Everything Flows", "lbl": null, "b": ["Heraclitus (c. 500 BCE, Classical era) argued the opposite: reality is fundamentally change and flux.", "Famous fragment: “You cannot step into the same river twice” — by the time you step in again, both you and the river have changed.", "For Heraclitus, the only constant in the universe IS change itself."], "c": [{"t": "KEY QUOTE", "b": ["“Panta rhei” (πάντα ῥεῖ) — Greek for “everything flows.”"]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Being vs. Becoming", "lbl": null, "b": ["Are you the “same” person you were 10 years ago? Would Parmenides and Heraclitus answer this differently?", "Which view feels more true to your own experience of reality — permanence underneath change, or nothing but change?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Categories of Existence", "lbl": null, "b": ["Aristotle (recall Unit 1!) proposed categories to organize everything that exists.", "Substance: what a thing fundamentally IS (e.g. “a tree,” “a person”) — it can exist independently.", "Property (or Attribute): what a thing HAS (e.g. “tall,” “green”) — properties can't exist on their own, only IN a substance.", "Example: “The tree is tall” — tree = substance, tall = property."], "c": [{"t": "KEY TERM", "b": ["“Substance” here does NOT mean “stuff” or “material” — it means the underlying “thing” that properties belong to."]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Universals vs. Particulars", "lbl": null, "b": ["Particular: an individual, specific thing — this apple, that chair, you.", "Universal: a general property or quality that many particulars can share — redness, roundness, justice.", "The Problem of Universals: do universals exist independently and objectively, or only in our minds, or only as patterns among particular things?"], "c": [{"t": "KEY CONNECTION", "b": ["Recall Plato's Theory of Forms (Unit 1, Lesson 3): Plato believed universals (Forms) are the MOST real things that exist — more real than the particular objects that merely “participate” in them."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Do Universals Exist?", "lbl": null, "b": ["Does “redness” exist somewhere, even if every red object in the universe were destroyed?", "Is “justice” more like a real thing waiting to be discovered, or more like a useful concept humans invented?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Modern Connection: Is Metaphysics Still Relevant?", "lbl": null, "b": ["In the early 20th century, the Logical Positivists (such as the “Vienna Circle” -  a group of European philosophers and scientists who pushed the idea of logical positivism) argued that metaphysical claims are literally meaningless — since they can't be empirically tested, they're not even false, just nonsense.", "Philosopher A.J. Ayer popularized this view in English-speaking philosophy.", "Critics respond: even science relies on metaphysical assumptions it can't fully test — that the universe is orderly, that causation is real, that other minds exist."], "c": [{"t": "KEY QUESTION", "b": ["Can you even argue that “metaphysical claims are meaningless” without secretly relying on some metaphysical assumptions yourself?"]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Does Metaphysics Matter?", "lbl": null, "b": ["Can a question be worth asking even if science can never fully answer it? Give an example.", "Modern physics debates multiverses, extra dimensions, and the nature of time. Is that physics, metaphysics, or both?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Where This Unit Is Headed", "lbl": null, "b": ["Today: the basic toolkit of metaphysics — being, becoming, substance, universals.", "Coming up this unit:", "Are minds the same thing as brains? What makes you “you” over time? Do we have free will? Does God exist? What is time, and are there other possible worlds?"], "c": [{"t": "THE THROUGH-LINE", "b": ["Be Curious First, Not Critical — metaphysics asks questions science can't fully settle. That's not a weakness of the field; it's the whole point."]}]}]}, {"u": 3, "l": 2, "title": "Plato's Allegory of the Cave", "sub": "What if everything you've ever perceived was just a shadow?", "cards": [{"k": "notes", "h": "The Allegory, Part 1: Life in the Cave", "lbl": null, "b": ["Plato tells this story in Book VII of The Republic (recall Unit 1!), as a dialogue between Socrates and Glaucon.", "Picture prisoners chained in a cave since childhood, facing a blank wall, unable to turn their heads.", "Behind them burns a fire; between the fire and the prisoners, people carry objects and figures, casting shadows on the wall.", "The prisoners have never seen anything but these shadows — to them, the shadows ARE reality. They even develop a whole “science” of predicting which shadows come next."], "c": [{"t": "KEY IDEA", "b": ["The prisoners aren't stupid. Given their evidence, believing the shadows are real is entirely reasonable. The problem isn't their reasoning — it's the limits of what they've been shown."]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "The Allegory, Part 2: Escape and Ascent", "lbl": null, "b": ["One prisoner is freed and forced to turn around. At first, the firelight is painful and blinding — his eyes are used to shadows, not real objects.", "He's dragged further, out of the cave entirely, into the sunlight. It's agonizing — he can barely see anything at first.", "Gradually, his eyes adjust: first shadows and reflections in water, then objects themselves, then finally the sun — the source of all light and life.", "The sun represents the Form of the Good — the highest, most fundamental truth, which makes all other knowledge possible."], "c": [{"t": "KEY CONNECTION", "b": ["Recall Unit 3, Lesson 1: Plato believed universals (Forms) are MORE real than particular objects. The Cave is Plato's story of HOW we might come to know the Forms."]}]}, {"k": "watch", "h": "“The Profound Meaning of Plato's Allegory of the Cave”", "lbl": null, "b": ["The Profound Meaning of Plato's Allegory of the Cave - https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4nHj3gL_JN0"], "c": [{"t": "WHILE YOU WATCH — JOT DOWN:", "b": ["What other ancient myth does the video compare to the Cave, and what do fire and the sun each represent in that story?", "According to Joseph Campbell's “Hero's Journey,” what are the three stages, and how does the video map them onto the allegory?", "The video names a modern film as a direct adaptation of the Cave. Which film, and why?"]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "A Universal Pattern: The Hero's Journey", "lbl": null, "b": ["The video places the Cave inside a much bigger pattern: the “Hero's Journey,” a story structure scholar Joseph Campbell identified across world mythology.", "Three stages: Departure (leaving the familiar world) → Initiation (trials and revelation) → Return (bringing wisdom back, often facing resistance).", "Mapped onto the Cave: Departure = being freed and dragged out; Initiation = adjusting to the sunlight and true reality; Return = going back to free the others — and being rejected.", "The myth of Prometheus follows the same pattern: the Titan steals fire (knowledge) from the gods to give to humanity, and is punished for it — fire plays the same symbolic role the sun plays in the Cave."], "c": [{"t": "KEY CONNECTION", "b": ["The video explicitly calls The Matrix a modern adaptation of the Cave allegory, and also draws parallels to the Buddha's enlightenment and other traditions — exactly the territory we're headed into next."]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "The Return: The Philosopher's Burden", "lbl": null, "b": ["The freed prisoner doesn't stay in the sunlight — Plato says he must go back down into the cave to free the others.", "Returning is painful too: his eyes, now adjusted to sunlight, struggle in the dark again. He stumbles, and the other prisoners mock him — his “vision” seems worse than theirs.", "Plato even suggests the prisoners might kill anyone who tried to free them and drag them into the light — foreshadowing the execution of Socrates, Plato's own teacher."], "c": [{"t": "WHY IT MATTERS", "b": ["For Plato, philosophers have a duty to return and share what they've learned — even though it's thankless, risky, and often unwelcome."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Would You Go Back?", "lbl": null, "b": ["If you were the freed prisoner, would you go back to the cave to free the others? Why or why not?", "Why might people resist being shown a bigger, truer picture of reality — even if it would ultimately help them?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Levels of Reality: The Divided Line", "lbl": null, "b": ["Plato paired the Cave with another idea: the Divided Line — four levels of awareness, from lowest to highest.", "Imagination (eikasia) — shadows, images, illusions", "Belief (pistis) — physical objects we perceive with our senses", "Thought (dianoia) — reasoning, like mathematics", "Understanding (noesis) — direct knowledge of the Forms themselves", "The Cave dramatizes this exact journey: shadows → objects → sunlight → the sun itself."], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Modern Caves", "lbl": null, "b": ["Recall Unit 1, Lesson 2: we already asked “what's a modern cave?” Today, let's go deeper.", "Algorithms and social media feeds can act like a modern cave wall — showing us a curated “shadow” version of the world, shaped by what keeps us watching, not necessarily what's true.", "Media literacy, propaganda, and echo chambers (recall Unit 2's groupthink!) all raise Plato's same question: how do we know the difference between the shadows and the real thing?"], "c": [{"t": "PREVIEW", "b": ["In a few classes, we'll watch The Matrix — a film built almost entirely on this exact allegory. Keep the Cave in mind."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Finding the Exit", "lbl": null, "b": ["What's one belief or assumption you have that you've never really questioned? How might you “test” it, the way the freed prisoner tested the shadows?", "Is it always good to leave the cave, or are there times ignorance might actually be easier, even if less true?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Modern Connection: On Screen", "lbl": null, "b": ["The Cave's central question — how do you know your reality is the real one? — is one of the most reused premises in modern film.", "Inception (2010): characters travel through layered dreams-within-dreams, never fully certain which level is “real.”", "Free Guy (2021): a background character in a video game world slowly realizes his entire reality is a simulation."], "c": [{"t": "TRY THIS", "b": ["If you've seen either film, identify the moment closest to the freed prisoner's first glimpse of the fire — the first crack in the illusion."]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Synthesis", "lbl": null, "b": ["The Allegory of the Cave isn't just an ancient story — it's Plato's whole theory of knowledge and reality in miniature: appearance vs. reality, the pain of learning, and the responsibility that comes with knowing more.", "In some versions of this story, the freed prisoner's mistake on returning is mocking the others instead of patiently helping them see. Recall our mantra: Be Curious First, Not Critical.", "Next lesson: the Mind-Body Problem — if the physical world can deceive us like this, what does that mean for the relationship between our minds and our bodies?"], "c": []}]}, {"u": 3, "l": 3, "title": "The Mind-Body Problem", "sub": "Are you your brain — or something more?", "cards": [{"k": "notes", "h": "Framing the Problem", "lbl": null, "b": ["Is the mind — your consciousness, yourself — part of the body?", "If it is: what happens to it after your body dies?", "If it isn't: what happens to it after your body dies?", "This is the Mind-Body Problem: how (if at all) do a physical brain and a non-physical-seeming mind relate to each other?"], "c": [{"t": "THINK ABOUT IT", "b": ["How do we define ourselves metaphysically (what we ARE), versus epistemologically (how we KNOW ourselves)? This lesson is about the first question."]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Dualism", "lbl": null, "b": ["Dualism: the mind and the body are two distinct, separate kinds of substance.", "Recall Descartes (Unit 1 & 2): the mind is a thinking, non-physical substance; the body is a physical, non-thinking substance.", "On Dualism, the mind could in principle survive the body's death — they're not the same thing to begin with."], "c": [{"t": "KEY QUESTION", "b": ["If mind and body are totally separate substances, HOW do they interact? How does a non-physical thought move a physical arm?"]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Monism", "lbl": null, "b": ["Monism: the mind and the brain are, ultimately, the same thing.", "There are two main types of Monism, each answering “what is that one thing?” differently:", "Materialism — everything is fundamentally physical/material.", "Idealism — everything is fundamentally mental."], "c": [{"t": "KEY TENSION", "b": ["For Monists, personal identity theory runs into a hard problem: if mind and body are one thing, what (if anything) could survive death?"]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Materialism", "lbl": null, "b": ["Materialism: nothing exists apart from the material world.", "What we call “consciousness” is just a function of the brain and the physical processes of the central nervous system.", "On this view, human beings are complicated physiological organisms — no more, no less."], "c": [{"t": "KEY IMPLICATION", "b": ["If Materialism is true, when the brain stops functioning, there's nothing left to survive — no separate “mind-stuff” to persist."]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Idealism & the Brain in a Vat", "lbl": null, "b": ["Idealism: there isn't actually a physical world — everything is in our heads.", "Philosopher Hilary Putnam illustrated this with the “brain in a vat” thought experiment: what if you were just a brain in a jar, having its neurons stimulated to produce the illusion of a normal life?", "How would you ever be able to tell the difference from the inside?"], "c": [{"t": "KEY CONNECTION", "b": ["Recall Plato's Cave (Lesson 2) and Descartes' evil-demon doubt (Unit 1): Idealism asks a very similar question — what if NONE of your perceptions correspond to an outside world at all?"]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Materialism vs. Idealism", "lbl": null, "b": ["If Materialism is true and consciousness is just brain activity, does that change how much your thoughts and choices “matter” to you?", "Could you ever prove you're NOT a brain in a vat? What would count as evidence either way?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Hume vs. Kant on the Self", "lbl": null, "b": ["David Hume: naturalistic — the entire contents of the mind, the self, and the conscious state are derived from experience and sensation alone.", "Immanuel Kant: more metaphysical — tried to define the self using both reason and religion.", "Kant placed great importance on the concept of an immortal soul — something Hume did not believe in."], "c": [{"t": "KEY CONTRAST", "b": ["Hume builds the self entirely from experience (bottom-up). Kant argues something structures experience from outside it (top-down) — recall his synthesis of Rationalism and Empiricism from Unit 2!"]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Built From Experience, or More?", "lbl": null, "b": ["Hume says the self is just a bundle of experiences and sensations, nothing more. Does that match how YOU experience being “you”?", "Does believing in an immortal soul (Kant) change how someone might live differently than believing the self ends at death (Hume)?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "The Problem of Life After Death", "lbl": null, "b": ["For Dualists, life after death is relatively simple to imagine: the mind is a separate substance, so it could persist without the body.", "For Monists, it's a much harder problem. If mind and body/brain are the same thing, what — if anything — could survive?", "Some Monists argue nothing survives (pure Materialism). Others look for a Monist account where consciousness could still, somehow, continue or transfer."], "c": [{"t": "PREVIEW", "b": ["Next lesson we tackle Personal Identity directly: even while alive, what makes you “you” over time?"]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Mind, Body & Death", "lbl": null, "b": ["Does it matter to you, practically, whether Dualism or Materialism is true? Would it change how you live?", "If you found convincing proof for either position, what would change about how you see yourself?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Modern Connection: On Screen", "lbl": null, "b": ["The Mind-Body debate shows up constantly in stories about artificial minds and digital consciousness.", "Ex Machina (2014): an AI's creator tests whether she has a genuine mind, or just a convincing simulation of one.", "Her (2013): a man falls in love with an operating system that has no body at all — raising the question of what a “mind” even requires."], "c": [{"t": "TRY THIS", "b": ["For either film, ask: would a Materialist and a Dualist disagree about whether the AI truly “thinks”? Why?"]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Synthesis", "lbl": null, "b": ["Today's map: Dualism (mind and body are separate) vs. Monism (mind and body are one) — and Monism splits into Materialism and Idealism.", "Hume and Kant offer two very different accounts of what the “self” even is, built from experience alone or from something more.", "Coming up: we're about to watch The Matrix — a film that puts almost every idea from today (and Plato's Cave) on screen at once."], "c": []}]}, {"u": 3, "l": "3B", "title": "The Matrix: Round Table", "sub": "A synthesis discussion — after our screening", "cards": [{"k": "notes", "h": "Quick Recap: The Concepts in Play", "lbl": null, "b": ["Plato's Cave — shadows mistaken for reality; a painful awakening; a difficult return.", "Dualism vs. Monism — is the mind separate from the body, or the same thing?", "Materialism vs. Idealism — is everything physical, or could it all be “in the mind”?", "Putnam's brain in a vat — could your whole experience of reality be simulated?"], "c": [{"t": "YOUR JOB TODAY", "b": ["For each concept, find at least one specific moment in the film that connects to it — you'll need this for the round table."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Reality & Perception", "lbl": "ROUND TABLE 1 OF 5", "b": ["The film repeatedly asks: what makes something “real” — what you can touch and see, or something else entirely?", "Recall Plato's Cave: the prisoners' shadows felt completely real to them, too."], "c": [{"t": "DISCUSS", "b": ["Is the simulated world “real” to the people living inside it, if they never find out the truth?", "What's the strongest piece of evidence a character in the film uses to tell the two “worlds” apart?"]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "The Choice to Know", "lbl": "ROUND TABLE 2 OF 5", "b": ["The central choice in the film — offered directly, with real consequences — mirrors the freed prisoner's forced awakening in the Cave.", "Unlike the prisoner, this character is given an actual choice, not forced."], "c": [{"t": "DISCUSS", "b": ["Would you make the same choice, knowing what coming to know the truth would cost you?", "Is choosing NOT to know sometimes the more rational choice? Why might someone choose comfortable ignorance?"]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Identity Across Two Worlds", "lbl": "ROUND TABLE 3 OF 5", "b": ["The protagonist has a body in one reality and a very different kind of presence in the other.", "This previews our next lesson: Personal Identity — what makes someone the same person across radically different circumstances?"], "c": [{"t": "DISCUSS", "b": ["Is the protagonist the “same person” in both realities? What would Locke (psychological continuity) say? What would Hume (bundle of experiences) say?", "If your mind could be transferred to a totally different “body” or setting, would you still be you?"]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Fate, Prophecy & Free Will", "lbl": "ROUND TABLE 4 OF 5", "b": ["A prophecy plays a major role in the plot — the idea that this character's path is somehow already determined.", "This previews our upcoming Free Will & Determinism lesson."], "c": [{"t": "DISCUSS", "b": ["Does believing you're “destined” for something change how freely you're actually choosing your actions?", "By the end of the film, does the story support fate, free will, or some mix of both? Defend your reading with a specific scene."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Doubt as a Method", "lbl": "ROUND TABLE 5 OF 5", "b": ["Recall Descartes (Units 1 & 2): radical doubt used as a METHOD to find what can't be doubted, not as a permanent state of confusion.", "The protagonist's arc mirrors this: doubting everything familiar in order to eventually reach a new kind of certainty."], "c": [{"t": "DISCUSS", "b": ["Where in the film does doubt lead to a WORSE outcome, not a better one? Does that complicate Descartes' method?", "Whose testimony does the protagonist eventually trust, and why? How does that connect to Lesson 3's discussion of evidence and justification (Unit 2)?"]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Synthesis", "lbl": null, "b": ["Today, one film gave us a working example of nearly every metaphysical idea from this unit so far: appearance vs. reality, dualism vs. monism, doubt, fate, and identity.", "This is exactly why metaphysics matters outside the classroom — these aren't just ancient puzzles, they're the same questions great stories keep returning to.", "Next lesson: we tackle Personal Identity head-on — the Ship of Theseus, and John Locke's theory of psychological continuity."], "c": []}]}, {"u": 3, "l": "3C", "title": "Inception: Round Table", "sub": "A synthesis discussion — after our screening (alternative to The Matrix)", "cards": [{"k": "notes", "h": "While You Watch: Look For's", "lbl": null, "b": ["No spoilers here — just things worth paying close attention to as you watch.", "Notice the specific technique characters use to test whether they're dreaming or awake — when do they use it, and why?", "Pay attention to how each dream “level” looks and feels different — what changes about the rules or physics at each layer?", "Watch for moments where a character's emotional state visibly affects the stability or content of a dream.", "Notice how time seems to pass at different rates depending on the dream layer — keep track of this.", "Listen for how characters talk about what separates a “real” memory from a false or planted one.", "Pay attention to the film's final shot, and how it raises (without answering) a question about certainty."], "c": [{"t": "KEEP TRACK", "b": ["Jot down the moment (roughly) each “look for” happens — you'll want specific scenes to cite in the round table."]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Quick Recap: The Concepts in Play", "lbl": null, "b": ["Plato's Cave — shadows mistaken for reality; a painful awakening; a difficult return.", "Dualism vs. Monism — is the mind separate from the body, or the same thing?", "Materialism vs. Idealism — is everything physical, or could it all be “in the mind”?", "Descartes' method of radical doubt — doubting everything to find what survives."], "c": [{"t": "YOUR JOB TODAY", "b": ["For each concept, find at least one specific moment in the film that connects to it — you'll need this for the round table."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Reality & Perception", "lbl": "ROUND TABLE 1 OF 5", "b": ["The film builds multiple layered realities, each feeling completely solid and convincing to the people inside it.", "Recall Plato's Cave: the prisoners' shadows felt completely real to them, too."], "c": [{"t": "DISCUSS", "b": ["Is a dream “real” to the person experiencing it, while they're inside it? Does it matter that they can't tell?", "What's the strongest piece of evidence a character uses to tell one layer of reality apart from another?"]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Identity Across Layers", "lbl": "ROUND TABLE 2 OF 5", "b": ["Characters behave differently, and sometimes appear differently, at different dream levels.", "This previews our next lessons: Personal Identity — what makes someone the same person across radically different circumstances?"], "c": [{"t": "DISCUSS", "b": ["Is a character the “same person” across every dream layer they enter? What would Locke (psychological continuity) say?", "If your mind could enter a fully convincing alternate reality, would “you” still be you there?"]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "The Mind Made Physical", "lbl": "ROUND TABLE 3 OF 5", "b": ["Dreams in this film are navigable, physical-feeling spaces that can be explored, built, and even fought in.", "This raises the Mind-Body Problem directly: what's the relationship between mental content and “physical” experience?"], "c": [{"t": "DISCUSS", "b": ["If a dream can feel completely physical, does that support Idealism (everything is ultimately mental), or does it just show how convincing the mind can be?", "Would a Materialist and a Dualist explain what's happening in these dream sequences differently? How?"]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Grief, Guilt & Distorted Perception", "lbl": "ROUND TABLE 4 OF 5", "b": ["A character's unresolved emotional state visibly destabilizes and distorts the shared dream world throughout the film.", "This suggests perception and “reality” (at least within a dream) are shaped by psychology, not just neutral fact."], "c": [{"t": "DISCUSS", "b": ["Should we trust a perception less if we know it's shaped by someone's grief, guilt, or desire? Why or why not?", "Is this different from how emotion might shape perception in ordinary waking life?"]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Doubt as a Method", "lbl": "ROUND TABLE 5 OF 5", "b": ["Recall Descartes (Units 1 & 2): radical doubt used as a METHOD to find what can't be doubted, not as a permanent state of confusion.", "Characters in the film use a specific personal technique, repeated throughout, to test whether they're dreaming."], "c": [{"t": "DISCUSS", "b": ["Is the characters' testing method closer to Cartesian doubt, or closer to Reliabilism (Unit 2) — trusting a reliable process rather than reasoning from scratch each time?", "The film's final shot deliberately avoids resolving its central question. Does leaving a question unresolved feel like good philosophy, or a cop-out? Defend your answer."]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Synthesis", "lbl": null, "b": ["Today, one film gave us a working example of nearly every metaphysical idea from this unit so far: appearance vs. reality, dualism vs. monism, doubt, and identity.", "This is exactly why metaphysics matters outside the classroom — these aren't just ancient puzzles, they're the same questions great stories keep returning to.", "Next lesson: we tackle Personal Identity head-on — the Ship of Theseus, and John Locke's theory of psychological continuity."], "c": []}]}, {"u": 3, "l": 4, "title": "Personal Identity I", "sub": "The Ship of Theseus, and Locke's answer.", "cards": [{"k": "notes", "h": "The Ship of Theseus", "lbl": null, "b": ["An ancient puzzle: a ship's planks are replaced one by one over time, as each rots away, until eventually every single plank has been replaced.", "Is it still the same ship? If not, at what point did it stop being the same ship?", "A twist: imagine someone collected all the OLD discarded planks and reassembled them into a second ship. Which one is the “real” Ship of Theseus — the continuously repaired one, or the one rebuilt from the original materials?"], "c": [{"t": "KEY IDEA", "b": ["This is a puzzle about Identity and Change: how much can something change, gradually, before it becomes a genuinely different thing?"]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Which Ship Is Real?", "lbl": null, "b": ["Give your own answer: which ship (if either) is the “real” Ship of Theseus? Defend your reasoning.", "Can you think of a version of this puzzle that applies to something other than an object — a team, a school, a country?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "From Ships to People", "lbl": null, "b": ["If a ship's identity is hard to pin down through gradual change, people are even harder — we change physically, mentally, and emotionally over an entire lifetime.", "How much can change before you cease to be “who you were” and become someone new?", "This is the central question of Personal Identity Theory: what makes you the same person over time?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Locke's Psychological Continuity Theory", "lbl": null, "b": ["John Locke proposed that personal identity is a matter of psychological continuity — not physical body, and not an immortal soul.", "Your identity is tied to your consciousness — your memory — and Locke argued that consciousness could survive even after death.", "If your consciousness could be transferred into another body, Locke argued you would simply continue: new body, same memories."], "c": [{"t": "KEY CONTRAST", "b": ["Recall Lesson 3: Locke's view isn't Hume's naturalistic materialism (self = physical body/brain) OR Kant's immortal soul — it's memory and consciousness specifically."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Testing Locke's Theory", "lbl": null, "b": ["If all your memories were copied into a clone of you, would Locke say the clone IS you? Would you agree?", "If you lost all your memories tomorrow (total amnesia), would you still be the same person, by Locke's theory? By your own intuition?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "An Objection: The Brave Officer Paradox", "lbl": null, "b": ["Philosopher Thomas Reid raised a famous problem for Locke's theory:", "An elderly general remembers being a brave officer in battle. That officer, years earlier, remembered being a boy punished for stealing from an orchard. But the elderly general does NOT remember the orchard incident at all.", "By Locke's memory-chain logic: officer = boy (connected by memory), general = officer (connected by memory) — but general ≠ boy, since there's no direct memory link."], "c": [{"t": "WHY IT MATTERS", "b": ["If identity requires a direct memory chain, this creates a logical contradiction: the general both is and isn't the same person as the boy."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Does Memory Alone Work?", "lbl": null, "b": ["Does Reid's paradox convince you that memory alone can't fully explain personal identity? Why or why not?", "Can you think of a memory you've lost, from earlier in a chain of memories that's otherwise intact? Does that feel like it changes who you are?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Modern Connection: On Screen", "lbl": null, "b": ["Few films dramatize personal identity puzzles as directly as these two.", "Memento (2000): the protagonist can't form new long-term memories, forcing him (and the audience) to ask what continuity of self even means without continuous memory.", "The Prestige (2006): a mystery built around duplication asks whether a perfect copy of a person could really BE that person."], "c": [{"t": "TRY THIS", "b": ["Would Locke's psychological continuity theory, or Reid's Brave Officer objection, better explain what goes wrong for the protagonist in Memento?"]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Synthesis", "lbl": null, "b": ["Today: the Ship of Theseus shows how puzzling identity-through-change really is, even for a simple object.", "Locke's answer for PEOPLE is psychological continuity — memory and consciousness, not body or soul.", "Reid's Brave Officer Paradox complicates this: maybe memory alone isn't enough.", "Next lesson: David Hume offers a very different answer — and you'll build your own physical model of it."], "c": []}]}, {"u": 3, "l": 5, "title": "Personal Identity II", "sub": "Hume's Bundle Theory — and your Identity Chain.", "cards": [{"k": "notes", "h": "Hume's Bundle Theory", "lbl": null, "b": ["David Hume argued the self is not a single, unchanging entity — it's a “bundle” of ever-changing perceptions, experiences, and impressions.", "When Hume looked inward for a “self,” he said he never found anything BUT particular perceptions — never a constant “I” holding them together underneath.", "On this view, “you” are less like a solid object and more like a river (recall Heraclitus, Lesson 1!) — constantly flowing, made up of different moments strung together."], "c": [{"t": "KEY IDEA", "b": ["Hume said that whenever he tried to catch himself without a perception, he could never observe anything but the perception itself — never a bare self underneath."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Just a Bundle?", "lbl": null, "b": ["Does Hume's Bundle Theory match your own experience of being “you,” or does it feel like something's missing?", "Recall Lesson 1: Parmenides (unchanging being) vs. Heraclitus (constant flux). Which one is Hume closer to?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Identity in Arcane", "lbl": null, "b": ["Personal identity questions aren't just ancient puzzles — they show up constantly in modern storytelling.", "In the animated series Arcane, one central character undergoes a profound identity transformation after trauma — even coming to be known by a new name.", "This raises a genuinely Humean question: is this still the “same” person under a new name, or has enough changed that a new “bundle” has effectively formed?"], "c": [{"t": "KEY CONNECTION", "b": ["This is the Ship of Theseus (Lesson 4) applied to a mind instead of an object: how much psychological change is required before someone becomes, in some sense, a different person?"]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Arcane & the Bundle", "lbl": null, "b": ["Think of a character (from Arcane or elsewhere) who changes dramatically after a difficult experience. Are they still the “same” character, philosophically speaking?", "Have you ever felt like a different person after a major experience in your own life? What changed, and what (if anything) stayed the same?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Introducing Your Identity Chain", "lbl": null, "b": ["Today you'll build a physical model of Hume's Bundle Theory: an Identity Chain made of paper strips, each one representing a different piece of who you are — a role, an interest, a value, a relationship, a goal.", "Linked together, the strips form a chain — not a single solid “self,” but a bundle of connected, individual pieces.", "Your teacher will hand out the full activity instructions and reflection questions."], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Modern Connection: On Screen", "lbl": null, "b": ["Hume's Bundle Theory — a self made of many shifting pieces, not one solid thing — has become a favourite premise in recent film and TV.", "Everything Everywhere All at Once (2022): a woman encounters countless versions of herself across parallel lives, each shaped by different choices and experiences.", "Eternal Sunshine of the Spotless Mind (2004): a couple erases specific memories of each other, testing how much of “who you are” depends on which memories remain."], "c": [{"t": "TRY THIS", "b": ["Would Hume say all those different versions are the “same person”, or a collection of related but distinct bundles?"]}]}]}, {"u": 3, "l": 6, "title": "Meaning & Free Will", "sub": "Does any of this matter — and do we choose it?", "cards": [{"k": "notes", "h": "Does Anything Matter? Nihilism", "lbl": null, "b": ["Nihilism: the view that nothing we do, nothing we create, nothing we love, has any meaning or value whatsoever.", "If there's no cosmic, built-in purpose to existence, some conclude that nothing ultimately matters.", "Axiom: a statement so evident or well-established that it's accepted without controversy. Many philosophical systems start from axioms — but what if “life has meaning” isn't actually one?"], "c": [{"t": "RECALL", "b": ["Lesson 1's Big Questions included: “Why is there something, rather than nothing?” and “Does any of this — even my act of questioning — actually matter?”"]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Atheistic Existentialism", "lbl": null, "b": ["Atheistic Existentialism: excludes transcendental, metaphysical, or religious beliefs from philosophical thought about existence.", "Existentialism is generally atheistic because it holds that each person's experience of reality is unique and only truly known by that person.", "Theists point to an objective reality and meaning; existentialists see only a subjective one — meaning isn't discovered, it's created."], "c": [{"t": "KEY CONTRAST", "b": ["Nihilism says there IS no meaning. Existentialism agrees there's no built-in meaning, but says you can still CREATE your own."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Created or Discovered?", "lbl": null, "b": ["Do you think meaning is something you discover (already out there, waiting) or something you create (built through your own choices)?", "Is Nihilism a depressing conclusion, or could it actually be freeing? Explain."], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Do We Have Free Will?", "lbl": null, "b": ["Having asked whether life has meaning, we now ask an equally big question: are we even free to choose how we respond?", "Biological/Genetic Determinism: the belief that human behaviour is controlled by an individual's genes or physiology — generally at the expense of the role of environment or choice.", "If determinism is true in this strong sense, are “choices” just an illusion — outcomes we were always going to reach?"], "c": [{"t": "RECALL", "b": ["Unit 2 introduced Fatalism (all events are predetermined and therefore inevitable). Determinism is a more precise, often science-based version of that same worry."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Are You in Control?", "lbl": null, "b": ["If your personality and choices are largely shaped by genetics and upbringing you didn't choose, how “free” are you really?", "Does knowing more about the biological causes of behaviour change how responsible you think people are for their actions?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Compatibilism", "lbl": null, "b": ["Compatibilism: attempts to blend free will and determinism — the belief that free will and determinism are compatible ideas, and it's possible to believe both without being logically inconsistent.", "As long as we're free from external coercion, we have “freedom of action” — even if there's a deterministic or causal connection between our will and our actions.", "This allows us to take responsibility for our actions: credit for the good, blame for the bad."], "c": [{"t": "KEY IDEA", "b": ["Compatibilists redefine “free” to mean “not externally coerced,” rather than “uncaused.” Under that definition, determinism and freedom can coexist."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Is Compatibilism a Real Answer?", "lbl": null, "b": ["Does Compatibilism's definition of “freedom” (not coerced, even if determined) feel satisfying, or does it feel like a clever redefinition?", "Give an example of a choice you'd call “free” under Compatibilism, and explain why."], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Modern Connection: On Screen", "lbl": null, "b": ["Free will vs. determinism drives the plots of many well-known thrillers.", "Minority Report (2002): police arrest people for crimes predicted before they happen — can foreknowledge and free will coexist?", "The Adjustment Bureau (2011): a mysterious organization actively steers a man's choices back onto a predetermined “plan,” whenever he strays from it."], "c": [{"t": "TRY THIS", "b": ["Would a Compatibilist say the characters in either film are still “free,” as long as nothing forces their choices directly?"]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Synthesis", "lbl": null, "b": ["Today we linked two of the biggest metaphysical questions: does anything matter, and are we free to choose how we respond?", "Nihilism, Existentialism, Determinism, and Compatibilism each offer a different combination of answers.", "Next lesson: we tackle the biggest metaphysical question of all for many people — does God exist?"], "c": []}]}, {"u": 3, "l": 7, "title": "Does God Exist?", "sub": "Four classic arguments — and their strongest counters.", "cards": [{"k": "notes", "h": "Natural Theology", "lbl": null, "b": ["Recall Lesson 1: Aristotle split metaphysics into three theories — Ontology, Universal Science, and Natural Theology.", "Natural Theology: the study of God, including the nature of religion, the existence of the divine, questions about creation, and related spiritual issues.", "Today we examine four classic philosophical arguments FOR God's existence — and the strongest objections to each."], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "The Cosmological Argument", "lbl": null, "b": ["Key proponents: Plato, Aristotle", "Every finite and dependent being has a cause.", "Nothing finite and dependent can cause itself.", "A causal chain cannot be of infinite length.", "Therefore, there must be a First Cause — something that is not itself an effect. What else could that be, but a god?", "COUNTER-ARGUMENTS", "(1) The argument may rely on circular reasoning — it assumes the First Cause needs no cause, without proving it. (2) Being a First Cause doesn't require being a sentient Being — the Big Bang could have “sprung into existence” without purpose."], "c": []}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Testing the Cosmological Argument", "lbl": null, "b": ["Do you find the “causal chains can't be infinite” premise convincing? Why or why not?", "If a First Cause exists, does anything actually require it to be conscious, intelligent, or “God-like”?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "The Ontological Argument", "lbl": null, "b": ["Key proponent: St. Anselm", "Favoured by rationalists — requires no external experiment or evidence, only reasoning.", "Anselm's core idea: if we can imagine a perfect, flawless being, with our imperfect minds, then that perfect being must also exist in reality — or where would we have gotten the idea that such perfection was even possible?", "COUNTER-ARGUMENTS", "This is an a priori argument that relies on presumptions — often the existence of a Supreme Being as one of them. It's also not impossible to imagine NOTHING existing a priori, including God."], "c": []}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Testing the Ontological Argument", "lbl": null, "b": ["Does being able to IMAGINE something perfect actually prove that it exists? Try this same logic on something other than God — does it still work?", "Is there a difference between the idea of a perfect being and the idea of a perfect island? Why might Anselm's argument work for one but not the other?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "The Teleological Argument", "lbl": null, "b": ["Key proponent: Thomas Aquinas", "“Teleology” comes from telos, meaning “purpose” or “goal.” It takes a “purposer” to have purpose.", "Where we see things obviously intended for a purpose, we can assume those things were made for a reason — in short, a design implies a designer.", "Aquinas argues the universe could not have been created by chance rather than design — therefore, there must be a Supreme Being: a designer.", "COUNTER-ARGUMENTS", "Complexity does not prove conscious design. Evolution explains how complex organisms arose from simpler ones over millions of years — and if design were the explanation, why weren't humans made perfect from the start?"], "c": []}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Testing the Teleological Argument", "lbl": null, "b": ["Does something being complex or well-suited to its environment prove it was DESIGNED, or could it just look that way?", "Can you think of something in nature that seems poorly “designed”? Does that count as evidence against this argument?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "The Mind-Body Argument for God", "lbl": null, "b": ["Key proponents: Leibniz and Spinoza", "Leibniz (in his Monadology): since we know mind and body both exist, something must be causing them to interact. God acts as the conduit connecting them — the invisible glue binding mind and body together.", "Spinoza's variation: “everything is God,” so there is no mind-body PROBLEM — mind and body are both simply aspects of the same underlying, single substance (God).", "COUNTER-ARGUMENT", "Most versions of this argument rely on God to work at all — a logical fallacy known as begging the question (circular reasoning). Remove God as the starting assumption, and the argument can't sustain itself."], "c": []}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Testing the Mind-Body Argument", "lbl": null, "b": ["Does Spinoza's “everything is God” actually SOLVE the Mind-Body Problem from Lesson 3, or does it just relabel it?", "Is “begging the question” (recall Unit 2!) a fair criticism of all four arguments today, or does it apply more to some than others?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Modern Connection: On Screen & On the Page", "lbl": null, "b": ["Questions of faith, design, and meaning show up in acclaimed fiction just as often as formal philosophical arguments.", "Life of Pi (Yann Martel, 2001; film 2012): a survivor offers two different accounts of the same events and asks the reader which story they'd rather believe — and why that might matter more than which one is literally true.", "Cloud Atlas (2012): lives and souls echo across centuries, suggesting a pattern or purpose connecting seemingly unrelated people."], "c": [{"t": "TRY THIS", "b": ["Is choosing to believe the “better story” in Life of Pi closer to the Ontological Argument (reasoning to belief) or something else entirely — a leap of faith?"]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Synthesis", "lbl": null, "b": ["Four classic arguments: Cosmological (First Cause), Ontological (imagining perfection), Teleological (design), and the Mind-Body Argument (God as connector or as everything).", "Each has real philosophical force — and each has a serious, well-known counter-argument.", "Next lesson: we leave the question of God and turn to time, space, and the possibility of other worlds entirely."], "c": []}]}, {"u": 3, "l": 8, "title": "Time, Space & Possible Worlds", "sub": "The shape of reality itself.", "cards": [{"k": "notes", "h": "Space and Time", "lbl": null, "b": ["Do space and time exist independent of human experience, or are they something the mind imposes ON experience?", "Recall Unit 2: Kant argued space and time aren't things “out there” in the world — they're categories the mind uses to structure experience.", "Contrast: Newton treated space and time as absolute, independently existing “containers” for events, regardless of any observer."], "c": [{"t": "KEY QUESTION", "b": ["If no minds existed anywhere, would space and time still “exist” in any meaningful sense?"]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Is Time Real, or Constructed?", "lbl": null, "b": ["Does the Kantian view (time is a mental category) or the Newtonian view (time is an independent container) feel more intuitively correct to you?", "Recall Parmenides and Heraclitus (Lesson 1): which view of time — unchanging or constant flux — lines up better with modern physics, as far as you know?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Necessity and Possibility", "lbl": null, "b": ["A necessary fact is true across ALL possible worlds (e.g. “2 + 2 = 4”, or “all bachelors are unmarried”).", "A possible fact is true in AT LEAST SOME possible world, even if not this one (e.g. “a world where you never learned to read”).", "Philosophers use “possible worlds” as a reasoning tool — not necessarily claiming these worlds physically exist, but as a way of testing what MUST be true versus what merely COULD have been different."], "c": [{"t": "KEY TERM", "b": ["Modal logic: the branch of logic dealing with necessity and possibility, built on exactly this “possible worlds” framework."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Necessary, or Just Familiar?", "lbl": null, "b": ["Give one example of something NECESSARILY true (true in every possible world), and one merely POSSIBLE (true in some, not all).", "Is it necessary that YOU exist, or merely possible? What would a world without you look like?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Cosmology and Cosmogony", "lbl": null, "b": ["Cosmology: the study of the universe — its overall structure, origin, and eventual fate.", "Cosmogony: the study specifically of HOW the universe came into being.", "Recall Lesson 7's Cosmological Argument: it's essentially a cosmogonical question (how did everything begin?) turned into an argument for God.", "Modern cosmology — the Big Bang, cosmic inflation, possible multiverses — increasingly overlaps with metaphysical questions philosophers have asked for millennia."], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Abstract Objects and Mathematics", "lbl": null, "b": ["Recall Lesson 1: the Problem of Universals asked whether qualities like “redness” exist independently.", "Abstract Objects and Mathematics asks a related question: can objects possess abstract properties, like a numerical value?", "Does the number 7 exist somewhere, the way a rock exists — or is it simply a useful pattern humans invented to describe the world?"], "c": [{"t": "KEY CONNECTION", "b": ["This is Lesson 1's ontology question returning at the end of the unit: what KINDS of things exist?"]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Do Numbers Exist?", "lbl": null, "b": ["If every mind in the universe vanished, would mathematical truths (like 2 + 2 = 4) still be true? Defend your answer.", "Does it matter, practically, whether numbers “really exist” or are just a useful human invention?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Modern Connection: On Screen & On the Page", "lbl": null, "b": ["Today's ideas — time, possibility, the shape of the universe — are exactly the territory of some of the most acclaimed recent storytelling.", "Interstellar (2014): dramatizes real physics like time dilation (time passing at different rates depending on gravity and speed) and higher dimensions, alongside deeply metaphysical questions about love and meaning.", "The Midnight Library (Matt Haig, 2020): a novel built almost entirely around possible-worlds theory — a library containing every possible life its protagonist could have lived, based on different choices."], "c": [{"t": "TRY THIS", "b": ["Which of today's concepts — necessity/possibility, or cosmology/cosmogony — does each of these best illustrate?"]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Unit 3, Start to Finish", "lbl": null, "b": ["L1: What is metaphysics? Being, becoming, universals. L2: Plato's Cave — appearance vs. reality. L3: The Mind-Body Problem, then The Matrix.", "L4–5: Personal Identity — Ship of Theseus, Locke, Hume's Bundle Theory. L6: Meaning & Free Will. L7: Does God Exist?", "L8 (today): Time, Space & Possible Worlds."], "c": [{"t": "THE THROUGH-LINE", "b": ["Be Curious First, Not Critical — an entire unit of questions science alone can't fully answer. That's exactly why they're worth asking."]}]}]}, {"u": 3, "l": 9, "title": "Unit Capstone: Interstellar", "sub": "Watch — then a round table discussion", "cards": [{"k": "notes", "h": "While You Watch: Look For's", "lbl": null, "b": ["No spoilers here — just things worth paying close attention to as you watch.", "Notice how time seems to pass at different rates for different characters — when does it slow down or speed up, and why?", "Pay attention to how characters justify their biggest decisions — listen for reasoning that sounds consequentialist, duty-based, or something else entirely.", "Watch for how the film visually or narratively tries to represent higher dimensions or non-linear time.", "Notice how distance — in space AND in time — affects the relationship between the main character and their family.", "Listen for moments where a character explicitly discusses fate, destiny, or how much control they really have over what happens next.", "Watch for how the film treats love, memory, or connection as something that might “transcend” physical laws — metaphysical claim, or something more poetic?"], "c": [{"t": "KEEP TRACK", "b": ["Jot down the moment (roughly) each “look for” happens — you'll want specific scenes to cite in the round table."]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Quick Recap: The Whole Unit in Play", "lbl": null, "b": ["Being, becoming & the Forms (L1) — what's fundamentally real, and does it change?", "Plato's Cave (L2) — appearance vs. reality, and the cost of seeing further.", "The Mind-Body Problem (L3) — what connects a person's inner life to the physical world.", "Personal Identity (L4–5) — what makes someone “the same person” across time.", "Meaning & Free Will (L6), Does God Exist? (L7), Time & Possible Worlds (L8)."], "c": [{"t": "YOUR JOB TODAY", "b": ["For as many of these as you can, find at least one specific moment in the film that connects to it — you'll need this for the round table."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Time & Relativity", "lbl": "ROUND TABLE 1 OF 5", "b": ["The film dramatizes real physics: time passing at different rates depending on gravity and speed (recall Lesson 8's discussion of space and time).", "This isn't just special effects — it's a genuine metaphysical claim about the nature of time itself."], "c": [{"t": "DISCUSS", "b": ["Does watching time pass differently for different characters change how you think about whether time is “one thing” everyone shares equally?", "Would Kant (time as a mental category) or Newton (time as an absolute container) have an easier time explaining what happens in this film?"]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Cosmology & Higher Dimensions", "lbl": "ROUND TABLE 2 OF 5", "b": ["The film asks viewers to imagine dimensions and structures beyond ordinary human experience — recall Lesson 8's “possible worlds” and cosmology/cosmogony.", "Some of what the film shows is speculative extrapolation from real physics, not established fact."], "c": [{"t": "DISCUSS", "b": ["Does the film's central cosmic mystery feel more like a Cosmological Argument (something must have caused this) or a scientific puzzle with a natural explanation?", "Is there a meaningful difference between something being “possible” in the modal-logic sense (Lesson 8) and something being physically possible?"]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Free Will vs. Destiny", "lbl": "ROUND TABLE 3 OF 5", "b": ["Several characters' paths feel simultaneously chosen and somehow inevitable — recall Lesson 6's Determinism and Compatibilism.", "The film raises a genuinely difficult question: can you choose something that was also, in some sense, always going to happen?"], "c": [{"t": "DISCUSS", "b": ["Find a specific decision in the film. Would a hard determinist, a Compatibilist, and a libertarian free-will believer each describe that decision differently?", "Does it matter, emotionally or morally, whether a “choice” was free, as long as it felt free to the person making it?"]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Love, Meaning & Physics", "lbl": "ROUND TABLE 4 OF 5", "b": ["The film explicitly floats the idea that love might be a force that operates beyond ordinary physical explanation — recall Lesson 6's Meaning and Existentialism discussion.", "This is a bold metaphysical claim, not just a sentimental line."], "c": [{"t": "DISCUSS", "b": ["Is treating love as something with real causal power a serious metaphysical position, or does the film just want it to feel true?", "Recall Nihilism and Existentialism (Lesson 6): does this film land closer to “meaning is created through connection,” or something else?"]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Identity Across Time", "lbl": "ROUND TABLE 5 OF 5", "b": ["Because of the film's time effects, some characters age at wildly different rates than people they love.", "This is a real-world version of the Ship of Theseus (Lesson 4): how much change, over how much time, before a relationship — or a person — isn't quite what it was?"], "c": [{"t": "DISCUSS", "b": ["When characters reunite after time has passed unevenly for them, are they still “the same” to each other, by Locke's psychological continuity theory?", "Does Hume's Bundle Theory (Lesson 5) make this kind of reunion easier or harder to make sense of, compared to Locke's theory?"]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Synthesis: Closing Out Unit 3", "lbl": null, "b": ["One film gave us a working example of nearly every metaphysical idea from this entire unit: time, space, identity, free will, meaning, and cosmology all at once.", "This is exactly why metaphysics matters outside the classroom — these aren't just ancient puzzles, they're the same questions great stories keep returning to.", "Next unit: we turn from questions of what's real to questions of how we should act — Ethics: Towards Right Action."], "c": []}]}, {"u": 4, "l": 1, "title": "What Is Ethics?", "sub": "From what is real, to what we ought to do.", "cards": [{"k": "notes", "h": "Defining Ethics", "lbl": null, "b": ["Ethics: from Greek ethos (character/custom) — the study of right and wrong action, and how we should live. Recall Unit 2, Lesson 2.", "Ethics doesn't just describe what people DO value — it asks what people SHOULD value.", "This unit builds a full toolkit for answering “what is the right thing to do?” — and for evaluating whether an answer is actually well-reasoned."], "c": [{"t": "THINK ABOUT IT", "b": ["Ethics is a branch most people already have opinions about. That makes it exciting — and makes “Be Curious First, Not Critical” more important than ever."]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Moral vs. Non-Moral Judgments", "lbl": null, "b": ["Moral judgment: a claim involving right/wrong, harm, fairness, or rights.", "e.g. “Stealing from a struggling small business is wrong.”", "e.g. “It's unjust to pay men and women differently for the same work.”", "Non-moral judgment: a claim about taste, convention, or plain fact — no right/wrong in the moral sense.", "e.g. “Pineapple doesn't belong on pizza.” (taste)", "e.g. “You should wear a coat, it's cold outside.” (practical advice, not morality)"], "c": [{"t": "TRICKY CASE", "b": ["“It's rude to chew with your mouth open” — is that a moral claim, or just a social convention? Reasonable people disagree. Some judgments sit right on the border."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Sorting the Judgments", "lbl": null, "b": ["Give one example each of a moral judgment and a non-moral judgment, different from the ones in your notes.", "Is breaking a promise always a moral issue, or does it depend on the promise? Where's the line?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Recap: The Three Frameworks", "lbl": null, "b": ["Consequentialism", "An act is right if it produces the best overall outcome.", "Deontology", "An act is right if it follows the correct moral duty or rule, regardless of outcome.", "Virtue Ethics", "An act is right if it's what a person of good character would do.", "This unit spends a full lesson on each — today is just the map."], "c": []}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Which Fits You?", "lbl": null, "b": ["Without overthinking it: which of the three frameworks sounds most like how YOU actually judge right and wrong day to day?", "Can you think of a decision where outcome-based thinking (Consequentialism) and rule-based thinking (Deontology) would give completely different answers?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Descriptive vs. Normative Ethics", "lbl": null, "b": ["Descriptive ethics: factual claims about what people or cultures actually believe or do.", "e.g. “Historically, many societies practiced slavery without widespread moral objection.” (a fact about the past)", "e.g. “In some cultures, arranged marriages are seen as honorable; in others, as restricting autonomy.” (an anthropological observation)", "Normative ethics: claims about what people OUGHT to do or believe.", "e.g. “Slavery is morally wrong, regardless of whether a society believed otherwise.” (a normative claim)", "e.g. “We ought to treat all people as having equal moral worth.” (normative)"], "c": [{"t": "KEY DISTINCTION", "b": ["Describing what people believe is NOT the same as saying they're right. Most of this unit lives in normative ethics — asking what SHOULD be true, not just what IS believed."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Is or Ought?", "lbl": null, "b": ["Give an example of a descriptive claim about morality (what people believe) and a normative claim (what's actually right) about the SAME topic.", "Recall Unit 2's Cultural Relativism: is cultural relativism itself a descriptive claim, a normative claim, or both? Explain."], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Three Levels of Ethical Inquiry", "lbl": null, "b": ["Metaethics: asks what morality even IS — is it objective, subjective, or something else? (Lesson 5 of this unit)", "Normative Ethics: asks what the correct moral FRAMEWORK is for deciding right from wrong. (Lessons 2–4)", "Applied Ethics: asks how to apply ethical theory to real, specific dilemmas. (Lessons 6–7)"], "c": [{"t": "THE MAP", "b": ["These three levels build on each other — metaethics asks if there's a right answer at all, normative ethics proposes theories for finding it, applied ethics tests those theories against hard real cases."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Where Do You Start?", "lbl": null, "b": ["Does it make sense to debate a specific dilemma (applied ethics) before settling on a framework (normative ethics)? Or does it not matter?", "Which of the three levels — metaethics, normative ethics, applied ethics — sounds most interesting to you right now, before we've even started?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Synthesis: Where This Unit Is Headed", "lbl": null, "b": ["Today: what counts as a moral question, descriptive vs. normative claims, and the three levels of ethical inquiry.", "Coming up: Consequentialism and Utilitarianism, Deontology, Virtue Ethics, whether morality is objective, and two applied dilemmas."], "c": [{"t": "THE THROUGH-LINE", "b": ["Be Curious First, Not Critical — especially here. Ethics is the branch most likely to make you want to defend your existing views instead of examining them."]}]}]}, {"u": 4, "l": 2, "title": "Consequentialism & Utilitarianism", "sub": "Judging actions by their outcomes.", "cards": [{"k": "notes", "h": "Consequentialism: The Core Idea", "lbl": null, "b": ["Consequentialism: an act is right if it produces the best overall outcome or consequences.", "Nothing is inherently right or wrong — the moral status of an action depends entirely on its results.", "Utilitarianism is the most influential form of Consequentialism: the right act is the one that produces the greatest good for the greatest number."], "c": [{"t": "KEY TERM", "b": ["Utility: a measure of overall well-being, happiness, or benefit produced by an action."]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Jeremy Bentham & the Hedonic Calculus", "lbl": null, "b": ["Jeremy Bentham (1748–1832) founded Utilitarianism, proposing that pleasure and pain are the only things that ultimately matter morally.", "He proposed a “hedonic calculus” — measuring an act's pleasure/pain by factors like intensity, duration, certainty, and how many people are affected.", "Example: choosing between two charities — one that helps 10 people intensely for a short time, one that helps 1,000 people mildly for years. The calculus tries to weigh these systematically."], "c": [{"t": "KEY CRITIQUE", "b": ["Can pleasure and pain really be measured and compared this precisely? Bentham's critics say the calculus looks scientific but is nearly impossible to apply in practice."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Can You Calculate Happiness?", "lbl": null, "b": ["Try Bentham's calculus on a real decision (e.g. choosing a weekend activity). Does breaking it into factors like intensity and duration actually help you decide?", "Is all pleasure equally valuable, or does the SOURCE of pleasure matter morally?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "John Stuart Mill: Higher and Lower Pleasures", "lbl": null, "b": ["John Stuart Mill (1806–1873) refined Bentham's theory, arguing not all pleasures are equal.", "“Higher” pleasures: intellectual, creative, or moral satisfaction (e.g. reading great literature, meaningful friendship).", "“Lower” pleasures: purely physical or sensory satisfaction (e.g. eating, simple entertainment).", "Mill's famous claim: “it is better to be a human being dissatisfied than a pig satisfied” — quality of pleasure matters, not just quantity."], "c": [{"t": "KEY CRITIQUE", "b": ["Who decides which pleasures count as “higher”? Critics worry Mill is smuggling in his own class-based preferences (favouring intellectuals like himself) rather than offering a neutral standard."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Are All Pleasures Equal?", "lbl": null, "b": ["Do you agree with Mill that some pleasures are genuinely “higher” than others? Give an example that tests your answer.", "Is ranking pleasures as “higher” or “lower” itself a form of moral judgment Bentham's original theory tried to avoid?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Act Utilitarianism vs. Rule Utilitarianism", "lbl": null, "b": ["Act Utilitarianism: judge EACH individual action by whether IT produces the best outcome, case by case.", "e.g. Lying to a friend about a bad haircut, if it produces more happiness than honesty would in that moment.", "e.g. Stealing bread when starving, if THAT specific act produces more good than harm.", "Rule Utilitarianism: judge actions by whether following a GENERAL RULE produces the best outcomes overall — even if breaking it might help in one case.", "e.g. Following “don't lie” as a rule, because a world where people generally tell the truth builds more trust overall.", "e.g. Following “don't steal” as a rule, because a society that respects property rights functions better overall."], "c": [{"t": "KEY QUESTION", "b": ["Act Utilitarianism can justify breaking a rule in one case. Rule Utilitarianism asks: what happens if EVERYONE treated that as permission?"]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Act or Rule?", "lbl": null, "b": ["Take the lying-about-a-haircut example. Would an Act Utilitarian and a Rule Utilitarian actually disagree here, or reach the same answer by different reasoning?", "Can you think of a case where Act and Rule Utilitarianism would give genuinely OPPOSITE answers?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Critiques of Utilitarianism", "lbl": null, "b": ["The “utility monster” problem: what if one being gained vastly more pleasure from resources than everyone else combined? Utilitarianism could justify giving everything to that one being.", "Tyranny of the majority: Utilitarianism can justify harming a minority if it produces enough benefit for the majority.", "Measurement problem: happiness and suffering resist precise measurement or comparison between different people."], "c": [{"t": "WHY IT MATTERS", "b": ["These critiques don't necessarily defeat Utilitarianism — but they explain why Deontology (next lesson) insists some actions are wrong regardless of good outcomes."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "The Trolley Problem, Revisited", "lbl": null, "b": ["Recall Unit 1's Trolley Problem: pulling a lever saves 5 people but kills 1. A strict Utilitarian would pull the lever. Does that still feel right to you now?", "Change the scenario: instead of a lever, you'd have to push one large person onto the tracks to stop the trolley, killing them to save 5. Does your answer change? Why might that be significant?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Synthesis", "lbl": null, "b": ["Consequentialism judges actions purely by outcomes; Utilitarianism (Bentham, then Mill) is its most influential version.", "Act vs. Rule Utilitarianism disagree about whether to judge individual actions or general rules.", "Utilitarianism's critiques — the utility monster, tyranny of the majority, measurement problems — set up next lesson's alternative: Deontology."], "c": []}]}, {"u": 4, "l": 3, "title": "Deontology", "sub": "Duty over outcome.", "cards": [{"k": "notes", "h": "Deontology: The Core Idea", "lbl": null, "b": ["Deontology: an act is right if it follows the correct moral duty or rule — regardless of its outcome.", "From Greek deon (“duty”). The most influential deontologist is Immanuel Kant (recall Units 1–3).", "Unlike Consequentialism, Deontology says some actions are simply wrong in themselves, no matter how good the results might be."], "c": [{"t": "THINK ABOUT IT", "b": ["Deontology trades Utilitarianism's flexibility for consistency — the same act is always right or always wrong, regardless of circumstance."]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Hypothetical vs. Categorical Imperatives", "lbl": null, "b": ["Hypothetical Imperative: a command that applies ONLY IF you want a certain goal.", "e.g. “If you want to pass the test, you should study.”", "e.g. “If you want to be healthy, you should exercise.”", "Categorical Imperative: a command that applies UNCONDITIONALLY, regardless of your goals.", "e.g. “Do not lie” — applies whether or not lying would get you something you want.", "e.g. “Keep your promises” — applies unconditionally, not just when convenient."], "c": [{"t": "KEY IDEA", "b": ["Kant argued MORAL duties must be categorical. If morality were only hypothetical (“don't lie if you want to keep friends”), it would stop applying the moment you stopped caring about the consequence."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Conditional or Unconditional?", "lbl": null, "b": ["Sort these into hypothetical or categorical: “Don't cheat on the test,” “Bring an umbrella if it might rain,” “Don't steal.”", "Can you think of a rule you follow that FEELS categorical but is actually hypothetical once you examine it?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "The Universalizability Formula", "lbl": null, "b": ["Kant's first formulation: act only in a way you could will to become a universal law — in plain terms, ask “what if EVERYONE did this?”", "Lying to get a loan: if EVERYONE lied to get loans whenever convenient, lending on trust would collapse — making the lie impossible to even attempt meaningfully. Contradiction.", "Breaking promises when inconvenient: if EVERYONE did this, promising itself would become meaningless, since no one would trust a promise."], "c": [{"t": "KEY TERM", "b": ["Maxim: the underlying principle or general rule behind a specific action — not the action itself, but the policy it represents."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Testing the Universal Law", "lbl": null, "b": ["Pick an everyday rule-breaking act (e.g. jaywalking, cutting in line). Does universalizing it lead to contradiction, or just to a worse world?", "Does Kant's test only catch LOGICAL contradictions, or does it also catch things that are merely bad ideas? Does that matter?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "The Humanity Formula", "lbl": null, "b": ["Kant's second formulation: treat humanity — in yourself and others — always as an end, never MERELY as a means.", "Using someone MERELY as a means: a scammer tricking someone out of money treats the victim purely as a tool for gain, with no regard for their interests.", "Treating someone as an end: a fair business deal, where both sides are honestly informed and freely agree, respects the other party's own goals even while both benefit."], "c": [{"t": "KEY DISTINCTION", "b": ["You CAN use people instrumentally — hiring an employee, for instance. Kant's rule is about never using someone MERELY as a means, without also respecting them as a person with their own ends."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Means, or Ends?", "lbl": null, "b": ["Give an example of treating someone as a means WHILE ALSO respecting them as an end (like the fair business deal). What makes it different from pure exploitation?", "Is advertising generally treating people as means, ends, or both? Defend your answer."], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Critiques of Deontology", "lbl": null, "b": ["Rigid rules can conflict: what happens when two duties clash — e.g. “always tell the truth” vs. “protect the innocent”?", "The classic “murderer at the door” problem: if a murderer asks where your friend is hiding, does the duty never to lie really require telling the truth, even if it gets your friend killed?", "Kant himself controversially argued yes — the duty not to lie is absolute, even here. Many philosophers find this deeply counterintuitive."], "c": [{"t": "WHY IT MATTERS", "b": ["This exact tension — rigid duty vs. terrible outcome — is precisely what Consequentialism was designed to avoid. Neither framework escapes hard cases entirely."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "The Murderer at the Door", "lbl": null, "b": ["Do you think Kant is right that lying is ALWAYS wrong, even to protect someone from a murderer? Defend your position.", "Could a modified duty (“don't lie to those who deserve the truth”) solve this problem? Would Kant accept a rule with that kind of exception?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Synthesis", "lbl": null, "b": ["Deontology judges acts by duty, not outcome — categorical imperatives apply unconditionally, unlike hypothetical ones.", "Kant's two formulations: Universalizability (could this be a rule for everyone?) and Humanity (never treat people MERELY as means).", "Deontology's rigidity in hard cases sets up next lesson's alternative: Virtue Ethics, which asks what a good PERSON would do, rather than which rule to follow."], "c": []}]}, {"u": 4, "l": 4, "title": "Virtue Ethics", "sub": "What would a person of good character do?", "cards": [{"k": "notes", "h": "Virtue Ethics: The Core Idea", "lbl": null, "b": ["Virtue Ethics: an act is right if it's what a person of good character — a virtuous person — would do.", "Founded by Aristotle (recall Units 1–3). Instead of asking “what rule applies?” or “what outcome is best?”, Virtue Ethics asks “what kind of person should I be?”", "Virtues are stable character traits — courage, honesty, generosity — built through habit and practice, not just individual choices."], "c": [{"t": "KEY SHIFT", "b": ["Consequentialism and Deontology both ask “what should I DO?” Virtue Ethics asks “what should I BE?” — and trusts that good actions follow from good character."]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Eudaimonia: Flourishing, Not Just Happiness", "lbl": null, "b": ["Eudaimonia (recall Unit 1): often translated “happiness,” but closer to “flourishing” or “living well.”", "Eudaimonia isn't a passing feeling — it's a state of living your whole life well, achieved through consistently virtuous action over time.", "For Aristotle, the point of ethics isn't to follow rules or maximize pleasure — it's to become the kind of person who flourishes."], "c": [{"t": "KEY DISTINCTION", "b": ["You could feel happy in the moment while living badly (recall Mill's “lower pleasures”). Eudaimonia is about the shape of a whole life, not a single feeling."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Flourishing vs. Feeling Good", "lbl": null, "b": ["Can someone feel happy moment-to-moment while NOT flourishing, in Aristotle's sense? Give an example.", "Is “living well” something you can only judge at the end of a whole life, or can you tell along the way?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "The Golden Mean", "lbl": null, "b": ["Aristotle argued every virtue is a MEAN (middle point) between two vice extremes — one of excess, one of deficiency.", "Courage = the mean between Recklessness (excess) and Cowardice (deficiency).", "Generosity = the mean between Wastefulness (excess) and Stinginess (deficiency).", "Proper Pride/Confidence = the mean between Vanity (excess) and Self-Deprecation (deficiency)."], "c": [{"t": "KEY IDEA", "b": ["The “mean” isn't a fixed mathematical midpoint — it's relative to the situation and person. What's courageous in a war might be reckless at a office meeting."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Find the Mean", "lbl": null, "b": ["Pick a virtue not mentioned in class. Name the excess vice and the deficiency vice on either side of it.", "Is it possible to have too MUCH honesty? What would the “excess” version of honesty even look like?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Practical Wisdom (Phronesis)", "lbl": null, "b": ["Phronesis: practical wisdom — the skill of correctly judging what the virtuous choice actually IS in a specific, real situation.", "Unlike Deontology's fixed rules, Virtue Ethics requires judgment — knowing WHEN honesty requires bluntness and when it requires tact takes experience, not a formula.", "Aristotle believed phronesis develops through practice and mentorship, not just theoretical study — you become virtuous partly by imitating virtuous people."], "c": [{"t": "WHY IT MATTERS", "b": ["This is Virtue Ethics' biggest strength AND its biggest challenge: it's flexible enough for real life, but offers no simple formula to check your reasoning against."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Judgment Without a Formula", "lbl": null, "b": ["Think of a real decision where “follow the rule” or “calculate the outcome” wouldn't have been enough — you needed judgment. What made it hard?", "Can practical wisdom be taught directly, or only developed through experience? What does that mean for moral education?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Critiques of Virtue Ethics", "lbl": null, "b": ["The circularity problem: a “virtuous act” is defined as “what a virtuous person would do” — but how do we identify a virtuous person, except by their virtuous acts?", "No clear action-guide: in a genuine dilemma, Virtue Ethics can struggle to say exactly WHAT to do, compared to Consequentialism's calculation or Deontology's rule.", "Cultural variation: what counts as a “virtue” has differed significantly across cultures and eras — does that undermine the theory, or just reflect real moral diversity?"], "c": [{"t": "WHY IT MATTERS", "b": ["Each framework's weakness is often the other frameworks' strength — which is exactly why philosophers still debate all three today."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Does the Circularity Problem Sink It?", "lbl": null, "b": ["Is defining virtue by “what a virtuous person does” genuinely circular, or is there a way out of the circle?", "If Virtue Ethics can't always tell you exactly WHAT to do, is that a fatal flaw, or does it just reflect how real moral life actually works?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Synthesis: The Three Frameworks, Compared", "lbl": null, "b": ["Consequentialism asks: what outcome is best?", "Deontology asks: what duty or rule applies?", "Virtue Ethics asks: what would a person of good character do?", "Each has real strengths and real weaknesses — and many real ethical decisions draw on more than one at once."], "c": []}]}, {"u": 4, "l": 5, "title": "Is Morality Objective?", "sub": "Moral realism vs. relativism, revisited for ethics.", "cards": [{"k": "notes", "h": "Framing the Debate: Realism vs. Anti-Realism", "lbl": null, "b": ["Moral Realism: objective moral facts exist, independent of what any person or culture believes.", "Moral Anti-Realism: there are no objective moral facts — morality is a human construction, attitude, or expression, not a discovery.", "This is a metaethical question (recall Lesson 1) — it's not asking WHICH acts are right, but whether “right” refers to anything objectively real at all."], "c": [{"t": "THINK ABOUT IT", "b": ["You can hold ANY of the three frameworks (Consequentialism, Deontology, Virtue Ethics) while still debating whether its judgments are objectively true or just useful human constructions."]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Moral Realism: The Case For Objective Morality", "lbl": null, "b": ["Moral Realists argue some moral claims are simply TRUE, the way mathematical or scientific claims are true — discovered, not invented.", "Common argument: nearly everyone treats “gratuitous cruelty is wrong” as more certain than almost any philosophical theory that might deny it.", "If morality is entirely non-objective, it becomes hard to say any moral progress (like the abolition of slavery) was actually an improvement, rather than just a change in preference."], "c": [{"t": "KEY QUESTION", "b": ["If two cultures disagree about slavery, does Moral Realism require one of them to simply be WRONG — not just different?"]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Testing Moral Realism", "lbl": null, "b": ["Was the abolition of slavery moral PROGRESS (an objective improvement), or just a change in preference? Defend your answer.", "If moral realism is true, how would we ever come to KNOW which moral claims are the true ones?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Moral Anti-Realism: Error Theory & Expressivism", "lbl": null, "b": ["Error Theory: all moral claims are systematically FALSE, because they assume objective moral facts that don't actually exist.", "e.g. “Murder is wrong” is false in the same way “unicorns have four legs” is false — not because unicorns have some other number of legs, but because there are no unicorns for the claim to be true OR false about.", "Expressivism: moral claims don't state facts at all — they express attitudes or emotions, like cheering or booing.", "e.g. “Stealing is wrong” really means something closer to “Stealing — boo!” — an expression of disapproval, not a factual claim."], "c": [{"t": "KEY QUESTION", "b": ["If Expressivism is right, can moral disagreements ever really be “resolved,” or are people just expressing conflicting feelings past each other?"]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Fact or Feeling?", "lbl": null, "b": ["Does “murder is wrong” FEEL like a statement of fact, or an expression of attitude, when you say it? Does that feeling settle the philosophical question?", "If Error Theory is right and all moral claims are false, should we stop making them? Why or why not?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Recap: Moral Relativism", "lbl": null, "b": ["Recall Unit 2, Lesson 5: Moral Relativism holds that moral judgments should only be assessed relative to a particular cultural or individual standpoint.", "Moral Relativism sits between Realism and full Anti-Realism: moral claims CAN be true or false — but only relative to a standpoint, not universally."], "c": [{"t": "KEY CONNECTION", "b": ["This is why Lesson 1's “descriptive vs. normative” distinction matters: Relativism starts as a descriptive observation (cultures vary) but is often used to make a normative claim (so no culture is more correct)."]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Divine Command Theory & The Euthyphro Dilemma", "lbl": null, "b": ["Divine Command Theory: an act is morally right if and only if God commands it — morality is grounded in God's will.", "The Euthyphro Dilemma (Plato, recall Unit 1): is an act good BECAUSE God commands it, or does God command it BECAUSE it's already good?", "Horn 1 (good because commanded): morality becomes arbitrary — if God commanded cruelty, cruelty would be “good.”", "Horn 2 (commanded because good): goodness exists independently of God — but then why is God needed for morality at all?"], "c": [{"t": "WHY IT MATTERS", "b": ["This 2,400-year-old dilemma still shapes debates about whether morality REQUIRES religion, or exists independently of it. Recall Unit 3's arguments for God's existence — this is a direct follow-up question."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Which Horn of the Dilemma?", "lbl": null, "b": ["If you had to pick one horn of the Euthyphro Dilemma, which feels less costly to accept — arbitrary morality, or morality independent of God?", "Does the Euthyphro Dilemma pose a problem for a believer, a non-believer, or both equally? Explain."], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Synthesis: Where the Frameworks Stand", "lbl": null, "b": ["Moral Realism: objective moral facts exist. Error Theory: moral claims are all false. Expressivism: moral claims express attitudes, not facts.", "Moral Relativism: moral truth exists, but only relative to a standpoint. Divine Command Theory: morality is grounded in God's will — challenged by the Euthyphro Dilemma.", "None of this tells us WHICH acts are right — it asks whether “right” means anything objective at all. That question shapes how confidently we can defend any ethical framework."], "c": []}]}, {"u": 4, "l": 6, "title": "Applied Ethics I: Artificial Intelligence", "sub": "Putting the frameworks to work on a real dilemma.", "cards": [{"k": "notes", "h": "Why AI Raises New Ethical Questions", "lbl": null, "b": ["AI systems now make, or heavily influence, real decisions: loan approvals, hiring, medical diagnoses, criminal sentencing recommendations, and split-second choices in self-driving cars.", "New questions this raises: Who is responsible when an AI causes harm? Can an AI BE biased? Does an AI need to be “fair,” and what would that even mean?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "The Self-Driving Car Dilemma", "lbl": null, "b": ["A modern Trolley Problem: a self-driving car's brakes fail. It can continue straight (hitting a group of pedestrians) or swerve (hitting a single pedestrian, or its own passenger).", "Unlike a human driver's split-second reflex, this decision must be programmed IN ADVANCE by engineers — turning a moral dilemma into literal code."], "c": [{"t": "KEY QUESTION", "b": ["Should engineers program the car to minimize total harm (Utilitarian), follow a fixed rule like “never swerve onto the sidewalk” (Deontological), or something else entirely?"]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Programming the Dilemma", "lbl": null, "b": ["How would a strict Utilitarian programmer solve the self-driving car dilemma? How would a strict Deontologist?", "Should a self-driving car ever be programmed to prioritize its OWN passenger's life over a larger number of strangers? Defend your answer."], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Applying the Three Frameworks to AI", "lbl": null, "b": ["Consequentialist approach: program the AI to calculate and minimize total harm across every decision (e.g. choosing the option with fewest expected casualties).", "Deontological approach: program the AI with fixed, inviolable rules (e.g. “never intentionally target a specific individual”), regardless of outcome.", "Virtue approach: harder to code directly, since AI has no “character” — but could ask “what rules would a wise, careful engineer build in, exercising practical judgment?”"], "c": [{"t": "WHY IT MATTERS", "b": ["Unlike a human being, an AI's “ethics” are fully explicit and auditable — every framework must be translated into literal rules or weighted calculations."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Which Framework Codes Best?", "lbl": null, "b": ["Which of the three frameworks seems EASIEST to translate into actual computer code? Which seems hardest? Why?", "Does translating ethics into code change the ethics itself, or just the format?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Moral Patients vs. Moral Agents", "lbl": null, "b": ["Moral Agent: an entity that can be held morally RESPONSIBLE for its actions — it can reason about right/wrong and choose.", "Moral Patient: an entity that deserves moral CONSIDERATION — it can be wronged or harmed — whether or not it can reason about morality itself.", "An infant is a moral patient (can be harmed, deserves protection) but not yet a full moral agent (can't yet be held responsible).", "Most philosophers currently say today's AI is neither a moral agent nor a moral patient — but this is actively debated as AI grows more sophisticated."], "c": [{"t": "KEY QUESTION", "b": ["If an AI can't be a moral agent, who IS responsible when it causes harm — the programmer, the company, the user, or no one?"]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Agent, Patient, or Neither?", "lbl": null, "b": ["Could a future, more advanced AI ever become a genuine moral patient — something that could actually be harmed, not just damaged? What would that require?", "If no one can be clearly blamed when an AI causes harm, is that a gap in our ethical thinking, or just an unavoidable feature of complex systems?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Bias, Fairness & Accountability", "lbl": null, "b": ["AI systems can learn and amplify biases present in their training data — e.g. a hiring AI trained on historical data reflecting past discrimination might replicate that discrimination.", "This raises concerns across all three frameworks: harmful outcomes for affected groups (Consequentialist), violating a duty of fair/equal treatment (Deontological), and whether deploying a known-biased system reflects practical wisdom at all (Virtue)."], "c": [{"t": "RECALL", "b": ["Unit 2's discussion of Reliabilism (Lesson 3) applies directly here: is a biased AI system a genuinely RELIABLE process for producing good decisions?"]}]}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Synthesis", "lbl": null, "b": ["AI ethics isn't a brand-new branch of philosophy — it's our existing frameworks (Consequentialism, Deontology, Virtue Ethics) applied to a genuinely new kind of decision-maker.", "The hardest new questions — moral status, responsibility without a clear human decision-maker, bias baked into data — don't have settled answers yet.", "Next lesson: a second applied dilemma, testing the same frameworks against a different kind of hard case."], "c": []}]}, {"u": 4, "l": 7, "title": "Applied Ethics II: Bioethics", "sub": "A second dilemma, the same three frameworks.", "cards": [{"k": "notes", "h": "What Is Bioethics?", "lbl": null, "b": ["Bioethics: the branch of applied ethics dealing with medicine, biological research, and questions about life, death, and the human body.", "Bioethics doesn't replace Consequentialism, Deontology, or Virtue Ethics — it applies them (and some field-specific tools) to real medical and biological dilemmas."], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "The Four Principles of Bioethics", "lbl": null, "b": ["Autonomy: respecting a patient's right to make informed decisions about their own body and care.", "e.g. A patient refusing a recommended treatment, even if doctors disagree — it's their body, their choice.", "Beneficence: acting in ways that benefit the patient and promote their wellbeing.", "Non-maleficence: “first, do no harm” — avoiding unnecessary harm.", "Justice: fairly distributing benefits, risks, and scarce medical resources."], "c": [{"t": "KEY TENSION", "b": ["These four principles can CONFLICT. Respecting a patient's autonomy (letting them refuse treatment) might conflict with beneficence (wanting what's medically best for them)."]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "When Principles Collide", "lbl": null, "b": ["Give an example (real or hypothetical) where Autonomy and Beneficence would give doctors conflicting advice.", "Which of the four principles feels most important to you? Is it always most important, or does it depend on the situation?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Case Study: Organ Allocation", "lbl": null, "b": ["A single donor kidney becomes available. Three patients need it:", "Patient A — youngest, best long-term prognosis.", "Patient B — has been waiting the longest.", "Patient C — has dependents relying on them.", "This is a real Justice-principle dilemma healthcare systems face constantly, with scarce resources and no way to help everyone."], "c": []}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Who Gets the Kidney?", "lbl": null, "b": ["How would a strict Utilitarian allocate the kidney? How would a strict Deontologist following a fixed rule like “first come, first served”?", "Is “youngest, best prognosis” a fair criterion, or does it discriminate against older patients? Defend your answer."], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Genetic Engineering: Treatment vs. Enhancement", "lbl": null, "b": ["Treatment: using genetic engineering to CURE or prevent a disease (e.g. correcting a gene mutation that causes a serious inherited illness).", "Enhancement: using genetic engineering to IMPROVE traits beyond normal healthy functioning (e.g. selecting for greater height, intelligence, or athletic ability in an otherwise healthy embryo).", "Many bioethicists who support treatment-focused genetic engineering are far more cautious about enhancement — worried about inequality, coercion, and loss of natural diversity."], "c": [{"t": "KEY QUESTION", "b": ["Is the treatment/enhancement line even stable? Is preventing a genetic predisposition to below-average height “treatment,” or “enhancement”?"]}]}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Where's the Line?", "lbl": null, "b": ["Give an example of a genetic change that feels clearly like “treatment”, and one that feels clearly like “enhancement.” Now try one that's genuinely hard to classify.", "If only wealthy families could afford genetic enhancement, would that change whether it should be allowed? Explain."], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "End-of-Life Care & Autonomy", "lbl": null, "b": ["Medical assistance in dying raises a direct clash between Autonomy (a patient's right to choose to end unbearable suffering) and other concerns — sanctity-of-life arguments, risk of coercion of vulnerable patients, slippery-slope worries.", "A strict Deontologist might hold that ending a life is always wrong, regardless of consent. A Consequentialist might weigh suffering reduction against risks. A Virtue Ethicist might ask what compassion and respect for dignity actually require in this specific case."], "c": []}, {"k": "discuss", "h": "Autonomy at the End of Life", "lbl": null, "b": ["Should a competent adult always have the right to choose the timing of their own death in the face of terminal illness? Defend your position.", "How might a Utilitarian, a Deontologist, and a Virtue Ethicist each approach this question differently?"], "c": []}, {"k": "notes", "h": "Synthesis: Unit 4 Wrap-Up", "lbl": null, "b": ["This unit built a full ethical toolkit: Consequentialism, Deontology, Virtue Ethics, the question of moral objectivity, and two applied dilemmas testing all of it against hard real cases.", "No single framework resolved every case cleanly — and that's the point. Real ethical reasoning usually draws on more than one tool at once.", "Next unit: we turn from individual right action to the ethics of society itself — Liberty, Fraternity, and Freedom."], "c": []}]}];
